  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/run-advisor"

  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/run-review" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
TASK_FILE="$1"
RESULT_FILE="$2"
STATUS_FILE="$3"
EVENT="$4"
SCHEMA_FILE="$5"
TITLE_FILE="$6"
# Which review lens this run is. Two independent lenses (correctness, security)
# run one after the other in this same pane, so the pane title and the log have
# to say which one PO is currently watching. Defaults for an old invocation.
LENS="${7:-review}"
STREAM_FILE="${RESULT_FILE%.json}.stream.jsonl"

log() {
  printf '[%s] %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')" "$*"
}

set_title() {
  # See the note in run-coder: without -t this retitles the active (Manager)
  # pane instead of this one.
  if [[ -n "${TMUX_PANE:-}" ]]; then
    tmux select-pane -t "$TMUX_PANE" -T "Advisor: $1"
  else
    tmux select-pane -T "Advisor: $1"
  fi
}

finish() {
  rc=$?
  trap - EXIT
  printf '%s\n' "$rc" > "$STATUS_FILE"
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]]; then
    set_title "review:$LENS done $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  else
    set_title "review:$LENS failed($rc) $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  fi
  tmux wait-for -S "$EVENT"
  exit "$rc"
}
trap finish EXIT

# Clear the previous round's status so its presence unambiguously means "THIS
# round finished". run-wait treats the status file as the single source of
# truth, and the Manager re-runs the Coder via resume and re-runs RuboCop after
# every fix, so a status left over from an earlier round would otherwise let the
# next wait report a stale result as if it were fresh. Done AFTER the trap is
# installed, so a failure from here on still records a status.
rm -f "$STATUS_FILE"

set_title "review:$LENS since $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
log ">>> Advisor $LENS review prompt sent."
# Plain `codex exec`, not `codex exec review`. Two reasons:
#   1. `exec review` is built around a scope flag (--uncommitted / --base /
#      --commit) that cannot be combined with a custom [PROMPT], and this
#      review is driven entirely from the prompt.
#   2. `exec review` silently ignores --output-schema (codex-cli 0.147.0): it
#      writes prose into the -o file, which merge-reviews then rejects as not
#      valid JSON. Plain `exec` honours the schema, as run-advisor does.
codex -a never -s read-only exec \
  --json \
  --output-schema "$SCHEMA_FILE" \
  -o "$RESULT_FILE" \
  "$(cat "$TASK_FILE")" |
  tee "$STREAM_FILE" |
  jq --unbuffered -r '
    if .type == "item.started" then "[started] \(.item.type // "item")"
    elif .type == "item.completed" and .item.type == "command_execution" then
      "[command] \(.item.command // "")\n[exit] \(.item.exit_code // "unknown")"
    elif .type == "item.completed" and .item.type == "agent_message" then .item.text
    elif .type == "turn.completed" then "[turn] completed"
    else empty end
  ' |
  while IFS= read -r line; do log "$line"; done
cat "$RESULT_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true
log ">>> Advisor $LENS review finished."
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/run-review"

  # classify-diff answers the two questions the review phase needs BEFORE it
  # spends a token: which lenses this diff actually needs, and which review round
  # this is. One helper, because both answers are wanted at the same moment.
  #
  # THE SKIP RULE, in one sentence - the same sentence the Manager prompt
  # states, deliberately duplicated so the rule cannot drift between the code
  # that applies it and the prompt that explains it:
  #
  #   A review lens is skipped ONLY when every path the change touches - both
  #   sides of a rename, and deletions too - is either a non-executable media
  #   file or a non-executable documentation file whose content this classifier
  #   read in full and found free of active content; anything else - executable
  #   code and any file git records as executable whatever its name, a document
  #   carrying a script, an embed, an event-handler attribute, a javascript: or
  #   data: URL, a leading shebang or an evaluable source block, a document too
  #   large, binary, deleted or unreadable to vouch for, shell scripts, config,
  #   dependency and lock files, CI definitions, env samples, database, migration
  #   and schema files, routes and API definitions, templates and views,
  #   generated runtime artifacts, symlinks and submodules, and any extension the
  #   classifier does not recognise - runs it.
  #
  # Fail-safe in both directions: an unrecognised extension RUNS the lenses, and
  # a path list that cannot be computed is an ERROR rather than an empty list -
  # an empty list would read as "documentation only" and would silently skip the
  # security lens on a change nobody ever classified.
  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/classify-diff" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

# THE SKIP RULE, in one sentence. The Manager prompt states the same sentence,
# and the two must not drift apart:
#
# A review lens is skipped ONLY when every path the change touches - both
# sides of a rename, and deletions too - is either a non-executable media
# file or a non-executable documentation file whose content this classifier
# read in full and found free of active content; anything else - executable
# code and any file git records as executable whatever its name, a document
# carrying a script, an embed, an event-handler attribute, a javascript: or
# data: URL, a leading shebang or an evaluable source block, a document too
# large, binary, deleted or unreadable to vouch for, shell scripts, config,
# dependency and lock files, CI definitions, env samples, database, migration
# and schema files, routes and API definitions, templates and views,
# generated runtime artifacts, symlinks and submodules, and any extension the
# classifier does not recognise - runs it.

if [[ $# -ne 7 ]]; then
  printf 'Usage: classify-diff <workdir> <scope-file> <round-file> <max-rounds> <baseline-file> <delta-file> <changed-files-file>\n' >&2
  exit 2
fi

WORKDIR="$1"
SCOPE_FILE="$2"
ROUND_FILE="$3"
MAX_ROUNDS="$4"
BASELINE_FILE="$5"
DELTA_FILE="$6"
CHANGED_FILES_FILE="$7"

[[ "$MAX_ROUNDS" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
  printf 'classify-diff: ERROR: max-rounds must be a positive integer: %s\n' "$MAX_ROUNDS" >&2
  exit 2
}
[[ -d "$WORKDIR" ]] || {
  printf 'classify-diff: ERROR: not a directory: %s\n' "$WORKDIR" >&2
  exit 2
}

# Clear the scope file FIRST, before anything can fail.
#
# This helper runs once per round, so by round 2 a scope file from the previous
# round is already sitting there. If the classification below fails, an absent
# scope file is the honest answer; last round's decision is not. Leaving a stale
# "docs-only / skip" behind for the Manager to read would turn a diff nobody
# managed to classify into one that looks like a decided skip - the same
# unreviewed-change-looks-reviewed failure as before, reached through the error
# path instead of the happy one.
rm -f "$SCOPE_FILE" "$DELTA_FILE" "$CHANGED_FILES_FILE"

# The path list is EVIDENCE, so a git failure stops here with a non-zero status
# and no scope file.
#
# Staged changes are listed as well as unstaged ones: the review covers the
# whole uncommitted diff, and a staged .rb file behind an unstaged README edit
# must never be able to look like a docs-only round.
#
# Three deliberate choices about HOW the diff is listed, each of which closed a
# real hole in an earlier version of this helper:
#
#   * NO --diff-filter. Filtering to ACMRT silently drops D, so a round that
#     DELETED app.rb while editing README.md classified as documentation-only
#     and skipped both lenses. Deleting code changes behaviour like any edit.
#   * --no-renames. With rename detection git reports only the DESTINATION
#     path, so renaming app.rb to docs/app.md looked like a single docs path.
#     Turning detection off splits a rename into a delete of the old path plus
#     an add of the new one, so BOTH sides reach the classifier.
#   * --raw, not --name-only, because --raw carries the FILE MODES. An
#     executable file is code no matter what it is called, and git records 100755
#     for it; an added runbook.md with a shebang is not documentation.
#
# core.quotePath=false keeps a non-ASCII path readable rather than \nnn-escaped.
git_out() {
  local label="$1"
  shift
  if ! git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" "$@"; then
    printf 'classify-diff: ERROR: could not list %s in %s\n' "$label" "$WORKDIR" >&2
    return 70
  fi
}

UNSTAGED_RAW="$(git_out 'unstaged changes' diff --raw --no-renames)" || exit 70
STAGED_RAW="$(git_out 'staged changes' diff --cached --raw --no-renames)" || exit 70
UNTRACKED="$(git_out 'untracked files' ls-files --others --exclude-standard)" || exit 70

# One "<mode> <path>" record per SIDE of every change. A git raw line reads
#
#   :100644 100755 <sha> <sha> M<TAB>path
#
# where the two modes are the file before and after the change; 000000 means the
# file does not exist on that side and carries no information. A line with no
# informative mode at all - an unmerged path, which git reports as 000000/000000
# - still gets a record, with an unknown mode, so it can never be classified by
# its name alone.
RECORDS="$(
  {
    printf '%s\n%s\n' "$UNSTAGED_RAW" "$STAGED_RAW" |
      while IFS= read -r raw; do
        [[ "$raw" == :* ]] || continue
        meta="${raw%%$'\t'*}"
        raw_path="${raw#*$'\t'}"
        read -r src_mode dst_mode _ <<< "${meta#:}"
        if [[ "$dst_mode" == "000000" && "$src_mode" != "000000" ]]; then
          # A DELETION. The record is emitted as its own kind rather than as the
          # pre-image's mode, because what matters is that this path's content
          # is being REMOVED and there is nothing left to vouch for.
          #
          # Emitting the pre-image mode instead used to rely on the worktree
          # file being gone to reach the unvouchable branch. That is not
          # reliable: a tracked document can be staged for deletion and then
          # RECREATED at the same path as inert untracked content, and the sniff
          # would read the recreated file, find no stage-0 index entry, and call
          # the whole round documentation-only. A raw record saying the path was
          # deleted is evidence about the change; an unrelated file appearing at
          # the same path afterwards is not a reason to discard it.
          printf 'deleted %s\n' "$raw_path"
        else
          emitted=0
          for side_mode in "$src_mode" "$dst_mode"; do
            [[ "$side_mode" != "000000" ]] || continue
            printf '%s %s\n' "$side_mode" "$raw_path"
            emitted=1
          done
          [[ "$emitted" -eq 1 ]] || printf 'unknown %s\n' "$raw_path"
        fi
      done
    # Untracked files have no git record yet, so the mode comes from the
    # filesystem instead - same three cases, same meanings.
    while IFS= read -r other_path; do
      [[ -n "$other_path" ]] || continue
      if [[ -L "$WORKDIR/$other_path" ]]; then
        printf '120000 %s\n' "$other_path"
      elif [[ -x "$WORKDIR/$other_path" ]]; then
        printf '100755 %s\n' "$other_path"
      else
        printf '100644 %s\n' "$other_path"
      fi
    done <<< "$UNTRACKED"
  } | sort -u
)"

# How much document this helper will read before it gives up and calls the file
# code. One number, not two, because a partial read is the worst of both: it
# costs the I/O and still cannot vouch for the tail. A document over the bound
# is not read at all and classifies as CODE. 1 MiB is far above any real
# markdown or org file and far below anything that would slow a diff of
# hundreds of documents down.
SNIFF_MAX_BYTES=1048576

# What "active content" means inside a document, as one case-insensitive ERE.
#
# Each alternative is something that EXECUTES or PULLS IN content when the
# document is rendered, exported or served - not merely something that looks
# like code. A fenced shell example is documentation; an <iframe> is not.
#
#   <script <iframe <object <embed <applet   HTML elements that run or embed
#   <svg                                     inline SVG carries the same DOM
#                                            that got .svg removed from the list
#   javascript: data:text/html               URL schemes that execute
#   on<name>=                                an HTML event-handler attribute.
#                                            Anchored to a preceding boundary so
#                                            that "configuration=" and
#                                            "monitoring=" do NOT match; without
#                                            that anchor any word containing
#                                            "on" before an "=" would fire, and
#                                            a false positive costs a whole
#                                            two-lens round.
#   #+begin_src #+begin_export #+call:       org-babel evaluates these on export
#   #+include: #+setupfile:                  org pulls in another file
#   .. raw:: .. include::                    reStructuredText passthrough and
#                                            file inclusion
#   include:: pass:[                         AsciiDoc inclusion and passthrough
#
# A shebang is handled separately below, because position matters for it.
DOC_ACTIVE_CONTENT_RE='<script|<iframe|<object|<embed|<applet|<svg|javascript:|data:text/html|(^|[[:space:]>"'"'"'/])on[a-z][a-z0-9]*[[:space:]]*=|^[[:space:]]*#\+(begin_src|begin_export|call:|include:|setupfile:)|^[[:space:]]*\.\.[[:space:]]+(raw|include)::|^include::|pass:\['

# Does this documentation file carry active content?
#
# Returns 0 - meaning "treat it as code" - when it does, AND whenever the
# question cannot be answered at all. Every failure mode here resolves the same
# way, because a document this helper could not read is a document it cannot
# vouch for, and "could not check" must never come out as "documentation".
#
# The file is read AS IT IS AFTER THE CHANGE, from the worktree. A file DELETED
# by this diff therefore has nothing to read and lands in the unreadable case.
#
# ONE materialised copy of a document. Returns 0 - "treat it as code" - when it
# carries active content AND whenever the question cannot be answered at all.
doc_copy_is_active() {
  local full="$1"
  local size nonnul

  # Not a plain readable file - deleted, a symlink, a directory, or unreadable.
  # No content to vouch for.
  [[ -f "$full" && -r "$full" && ! -L "$full" ]] || return 0

  size="$(wc -c < "$full" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  size="${size//[[:space:]]/}"
  [[ "$size" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 0
  [[ "$size" -le "$SNIFF_MAX_BYTES" ]] || return 0

  # Binary content is not documentation, and a text pattern scan over it means
  # nothing. A NUL byte anywhere is the practical test, and it is why media is
  # never routed through this function.
  nonnul="$(LC_ALL=C tr -d '\000' < "$full" 2>/dev/null | wc -c || true)"
  nonnul="${nonnul//[[:space:]]/}"
  [[ "$nonnul" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 0
  [[ "$size" -eq "$nonnul" ]] || return 0

  # A shebang counts on the FIRST LINE only - byte 0 is the one position where
  # it makes the file runnable. Further down it is a code sample in a tutorial,
  # and a file that is actually executable was already caught by the 100755
  # mode gate in is_docs_or_media.
  [[ "$(head -c 2 "$full" 2>/dev/null || true)" != '#!' ]] || return 0

  if LC_ALL=C grep -qiE "$DOC_ACTIVE_CONTENT_RE" -- "$full" 2>/dev/null; then
    return 0
  fi
  return 1
}

# Does this documentation path carry active content on EITHER SIDE of the
# uncommitted change?
#
# The worktree copy is not the whole change. A path can be STAGED carrying a
# <script> and then edited back to inert prose in the worktree: the staged blob
# is genuinely part of what is about to be committed, but a worktree-only sniff
# sees the inert copy and can mark the whole round documentation-only. So both
# sides are read, and the path is code if EITHER of them is active or
# unvouchable. Nothing that is part of the uncommitted change may be invisible
# to this check.
doc_carries_active_content() {
  local path="$1"
  local staged_tmp

  # WORKTREE side.
  if doc_copy_is_active "$WORKDIR/$path"; then
    return 0
  fi

  # INDEX side, and ONLY when the path really has a staged entry. An untracked
  # file has no index entry, and that absence is not a failure - it means there
  # is no second copy to read, not that a copy could not be read. Treating it as
  # unvouchable would make every untracked document code and delete the
  # documentation-only case outright.
  git -C "$WORKDIR" cat-file -e ":$path" 2>/dev/null || return 1

  staged_tmp="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/classify-staged.XXXXXX")" || return 0
  if ! git -C "$WORKDIR" show ":$path" > "$staged_tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
    rm -f "$staged_tmp"
    return 0
  fi
  if doc_copy_is_active "$staged_tmp"; then
    rm -f "$staged_tmp"
    return 0
  fi
  rm -f "$staged_tmp"
  return 1
}

# Documentation and media ONLY, and only for an ordinary non-executable file.
# Everything else - including every extension not named here - is code as far as
# this classifier is concerned.
is_docs_or_media() {
  local mode="$1" path="$2" base ext

  # MODE FIRST, before the name is even looked at. An executable file is code
  # however it is named, and so is a symlink (120000) or a submodule (160000),
  # whose real content this classifier cannot see, and so is the synthetic
  # "deleted" and "unknown" the record builder emits. Only a plain 100644 file
  # can go on to be judged by its extension and its content.
  [[ "$mode" == "100644" ]] || return 1

  base="${path##*/}"

  # Names that look like documentation by extension but are dependency or build
  # inputs. Checked FIRST so they never reach the docs list below.
  case "$base" in
    requirements*.txt|constraints*.txt|CMakeLists.txt) return 1 ;;
  esac

  ext=""
  [[ "$base" == *.* ]] && ext="${base##*.}"
  ext="$(printf '%s' "$ext" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"

  # The test every entry below has to pass: can this format carry executable or
  # active content BY SPECIFICATION? If it can, it is code, not documentation.
  # Five extensions failed that test outright and are deliberately NOT here:
  #
  #   mdx      compiled as a JavaScript module - JSX and imports execute
  #   svg      an XML DOM that may carry <script>, event handlers, foreignObject
  #   pdf      supports embedded JavaScript actions
  #   ai       a PDF/PostScript container, so it inherits the same
  #   html/htm  never listed, and must not be
  #
  # Text markup (md, rst, adoc, org, txt) stays on the list, but its EXTENSION
  # IS NO LONGER SUFFICIENT: all of it permits raw HTML, and org evaluates
  # source blocks on export under common configurations. So the content decides,
  # via doc_carries_active_content below. Removing these extensions instead
  # would have deleted the documentation-only case rather than tightened it.
  case "$ext" in
    md|markdown|rst|adoc|asciidoc|org|txt|text)
      if doc_carries_active_content "$path"; then
        return 1
      fi
      return 0
      ;;
    # MEDIA is judged by extension alone, and is deliberately NOT sniffed. These
    # formats have no script engine, so there is no active content to find; the
    # ones that DO have one - svg, pdf, ai - are absent from this list entirely.
    # A binary-content test would also reject every one of them out of hand.
    png|jpg|jpeg|gif|bmp|tif|tiff|ico|webp|avif|heic) return 0 ;;
    mp3|wav|ogg|flac|m4a|mp4|mov|webm|avi|mkv) return 0 ;;
    psd|sketch|fig) return 0 ;;
  esac
  case "$base" in
    LICENSE|LICENCE|COPYING|NOTICE|AUTHORS|CONTRIBUTORS|README|CHANGELOG|CHANGES|CONTRIBUTING)
      # Extensionless prose, sniffed on exactly the same terms as .md.
      if doc_carries_active_content "$path"; then
        return 1
      fi
      return 0
      ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# A PATH is code when ANY record for it is code. A file renamed from app.rb to
# docs/app.md contributes a record for each side, and the .rb side alone is
# enough to run the lenses.
ALL_PATHS=""
CODE_PATHS=""
while IFS=' ' read -r record_mode record_path; do
  [[ -n "$record_path" ]] || continue
  ALL_PATHS="$ALL_PATHS$record_path"$'\n'
  is_docs_or_media "$record_mode" "$record_path" ||
    CODE_PATHS="$CODE_PATHS$record_path"$'\n'
done <<< "$RECORDS"

count_paths() {
  printf '%s' "$1" | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d' | sort -u | wc -l | tr -d '[:space:]'
}

TOTAL="$(count_paths "$ALL_PATHS")"
CODE="$(count_paths "$CODE_PATHS")"
DOCS=$((TOTAL - CODE))
FIRST_CODE_PATH="$(
  printf '%s' "$CODE_PATHS" | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d' | sort -u | head -n 1
)"

if [[ "$TOTAL" -eq 0 ]]; then
  # NOT "docs only": nothing was classified at all. Running both lenses is the
  # fail-safe answer, and an empty diff is itself something PO needs to hear.
  SCOPE="no-changes"
  CORRECTNESS="run"
  SECURITY="run"
elif [[ "$CODE" -eq 0 ]]; then
  SCOPE="docs-only"
  CORRECTNESS="skip"
  SECURITY="skip"
else
  SCOPE="code"
  CORRECTNESS="run"
  SECURITY="run"
fi

# The round counter lives in a file, not in the Manager's head, so the hard cap
# survives a compaction or a resume.
ROUND=0
if [[ -s "$ROUND_FILE" ]]; then
  ROUND="$(head -n 1 "$ROUND_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  [[ "$ROUND" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || ROUND=0
fi
ROUND=$((ROUND + 1))
printf '%s\n' "$ROUND" > "$ROUND_FILE"

if [[ "$ROUND" -ge "$MAX_ROUNDS" ]]; then
  FINAL_ROUND="yes"
else
  FINAL_ROUND="no"
fi

# The FULL changed-file name list, always, in every round including delta ones.
#
# It is a few hundred bytes and it is what lets a lens notice that the delta
# touches something it ought to read more of. A delta without it is a blindfold:
# the reviewer cannot tell whether the three lines it was handed sit in a file
# the earlier change rewrote completely.
printf '%s\n' "$ALL_PATHS" | sed '/^[[:space:]]*$/d' | sort -u > "$CHANGED_FILES_FILE"

# --- Review scope: full diff, or only the delta since the last review? --------
#
# Round 1 is ALWAYS the full uncommitted diff. Later rounds exist to confirm that
# specific findings were fixed and that the fix broke nothing, so they read the
# delta since the state round N-1 actually reviewed.
#
# EVERY failure here falls back to the FULL diff. There is no path that reviews
# less than the full change unless a delta was computed successfully and came
# back non-empty. That single rule is the whole safety argument, so each branch
# below records WHY, and the reason travels to the Manager in the scope file.
REVIEW_MODE="full"
DELTA_REASON="round 1: the first review of a run always reads the full uncommitted diff"
BASELINE=""

if [[ "$ROUND" -gt 1 ]]; then
  REVIEW_MODE="full"
  DELTA_REASON="no usable review baseline was found, so this round re-reads the full diff"
  if [[ -s "$BASELINE_FILE" ]]; then
    BASELINE="$(head -n 1 "$BASELINE_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  fi

  if [[ -z "$BASELINE" ]]; then
    DELTA_REASON="no review baseline was recorded for the previous round (a fresh run, a resume, or a failed capture), so this round reads the full diff"
  elif ! [[ "$BASELINE" =~ ^[0-9a-fA-F]{7,64}$ ]]; then
    DELTA_REASON="the recorded review baseline is not a git object id, so this round reads the full diff"
  elif ! git -C "$WORKDIR" cat-file -e "${BASELINE}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
    DELTA_REASON="the recorded review baseline $BASELINE is not a commit in this repository, so this round reads the full diff"
  else
    # Two halves, and the second one matters more than it looks.
    #
    # `git diff <baseline> --` covers TRACKED changes only. `git stash create`
    # does not record untracked files, so a file that is untracked now appears
    # in NEITHER the baseline NOR that diff - a brand new source file the Coder
    # just wrote would be invisible to a delta review. So every currently
    # untracked file is appended as a full addition.
    #
    # DOCUMENTED CONSEQUENCE: an untracked file that stays untracked reappears
    # in full in EVERY delta, round after round. That is deliberate. It is the
    # fail-safe direction - reviewed again rather than missed - and the
    # alternative would need the index touched, which is forbidden here.
    # The TRACKED half is computed first and ON ITS OWN, because it is the only
    # honest answer to "did any new work land since the last review?".
    #
    # The emptiness test MUST be made here, before untracked files are appended.
    # Untracked files reappear in every delta by construction, so a delta that
    # has already been padded with them is never empty and the "nothing changed"
    # signal would never fire - measured, not assumed.
    DELTA_TMP="$(mktemp "${DELTA_FILE}.XXXXXX")"
    DELTA_OK=true
    if ! git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" diff "$BASELINE" -- > "$DELTA_TMP" 2>/dev/null; then
      DELTA_OK=false
    fi

    # `git diff <baseline> --` above compares the baseline against the WORKTREE,
    # so anything that exists only in the INDEX is absent from it. Staging a
    # change and then putting the worktree copy back to the already-reviewed
    # text produced a delta that omitted the staged content entirely, while the
    # uncommitted change still carried it.
    #
    # The staged side is added for a divergent path - one where the index and
    # the worktree disagree, so the worktree diff above cannot already represent
    # the index - when EITHER of these holds:
    #
    #   A. the index differs from HEAD, so there is genuinely STAGED content
    #      rather than an index that merely lags behind an unstaged edit; or
    #   B. the index differs from THE INDEX AS IT WAS AT THE LAST REVIEW, so the
    #      index has moved since a lens looked at it. That is how a previously
    #      reviewed fix gets removed through the index alone - staged before the
    #      review, reset back to HEAD after it - without any later round seeing
    #      it go.
    #
    # B is measured against the baseline's own record of the index, not against
    # HEAD and not against the baseline tree. `git stash create` builds a merge
    # commit whose SECOND PARENT is a commit of the index at capture time, so
    # <baseline>^2 is exactly "the index the previous review ran against".
    #
    # Comparing to the baseline TREE instead was tried and is wrong: in normal
    # operation the Coder edits the worktree and stages nothing, so the index
    # sits at HEAD while the baseline tree holds the reviewed work. That test is
    # therefore TRUE for every quiet path in every ordinary round - it put a
    # reverse diff back to the committed text into every delta, and it broke the
    # empty-delta fallback outright by manufacturing content out of a round in
    # which nothing had happened. Measured, not assumed. Comparing index to
    # index has neither problem: an index that has not moved says nothing, and
    # an index that has moved is exactly the event worth showing.
    #
    # This runs BEFORE the emptiness test on purpose. A fix that was staged and
    # not left in the worktree is real work, and it must count as a non-empty
    # delta rather than falling back to a full re-read.
    if [[ "$DELTA_OK" == "true" ]]; then
      # An unborn HEAD has nothing to compare the index against. Rather than
      # guess, every divergent path is taken - more content, never less.
      if git -C "$WORKDIR" rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        STAGED_PATHS="$(git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" diff --cached --name-only 2>/dev/null || true)"
      else
        STAGED_PATHS=""
        STAGED_ALL=true
      fi
      # The index the last review ran against. If it cannot be resolved, rule B
      # cannot be evaluated, so every divergent path is taken instead.
      BASELINE_INDEX="$(git -C "$WORKDIR" rev-parse --verify --quiet "${BASELINE}^2" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      DIVERGENT_PATHS="$(git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" diff --name-only 2>/dev/null || true)"
      while IFS= read -r divergent_path; do
        [[ -n "$divergent_path" ]] || continue
        if [[ "${STAGED_ALL:-false}" != "true" ]]; then
          if ! printf '%s\n' "$STAGED_PATHS" | grep -Fxq -- "$divergent_path"; then
            # Not staged against HEAD. Take it only under rule B: has the index
            # moved since the review?
            if [[ -n "$BASELINE_INDEX" ]]; then
              set +e
              git -C "$WORKDIR" diff --cached --quiet "$BASELINE_INDEX" -- "$divergent_path" 2>/dev/null
              index_moved=$?
              set -e
              # --quiet exits 0 when there is no difference and 1 when there is;
              # anything above 1 is a real error and is treated as "include it".
              [[ "$index_moved" -ne 0 ]] || continue
            fi
          fi
        fi
        if ! git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" diff --cached "$BASELINE" -- "$divergent_path" >> "$DELTA_TMP" 2>/dev/null; then
          DELTA_OK=false
          break
        fi
      done <<< "$DIVERGENT_PATHS"
    fi

    if [[ "$DELTA_OK" != "true" ]]; then
      rm -f "$DELTA_TMP"
      DELTA_REASON="the delta since baseline $BASELINE could not be computed, so this round reads the full diff"
    elif [[ ! -s "$DELTA_TMP" ]]; then
      # No tracked file changed since the state the last review read. Whatever
      # the Coder reported, no fix landed anywhere this baseline can see, and
      # silence is not a pass. A change that created ONLY a new untracked file
      # also lands here, and re-reading the full diff is the safe answer.
      rm -f "$DELTA_TMP"
      DELTA_REASON="the delta since baseline $BASELINE came back EMPTY - no tracked file changed since the last review - so this round reads the full diff"
    else
      # Only now append the untracked files, so they enrich a real delta rather
      # than manufacture one.
      while IFS= read -r untracked_path; do
        [[ -n "$untracked_path" ]] || continue
        # --no-index exits 0 when the files match and 1 when they differ; only
        # 2 and above are real errors.
        set +e
        git -c core.quotePath=false -C "$WORKDIR" diff --no-index -- /dev/null "$untracked_path" >> "$DELTA_TMP" 2>/dev/null
        untracked_rc=$?
        set -e
        if [[ "$untracked_rc" -gt 1 ]]; then
          DELTA_OK=false
          break
        fi
      done <<< "$UNTRACKED"

      if [[ "$DELTA_OK" != "true" ]]; then
        rm -f "$DELTA_TMP"
        DELTA_REASON="an untracked file could not be added to the delta since baseline $BASELINE, so this round reads the full diff"
      else
        mv "$DELTA_TMP" "$DELTA_FILE"
        REVIEW_MODE="delta"
        DELTA_REASON="reviewing only what changed since the state round $((ROUND - 1)) reviewed (baseline $BASELINE)"
      fi
    fi
  fi
fi

# Written to a temp file and renamed into place, so the Manager can only ever
# read a COMPLETE scope file. A half-written one - a full disk, a killed pane -
# could be missing the very line that says a lens must run.
SCOPE_TMP="$(mktemp "${SCOPE_FILE}.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$SCOPE_TMP"' EXIT
{
  printf 'round=%s\n' "$ROUND"
  printf 'max_rounds=%s\n' "$MAX_ROUNDS"
  printf 'final_round=%s\n' "$FINAL_ROUND"
  printf 'scope=%s\n' "$SCOPE"
  printf 'correctness=%s\n' "$CORRECTNESS"
  printf 'security=%s\n' "$SECURITY"
  printf 'paths=%s\n' "$TOTAL"
  printf 'docs_paths=%s\n' "$DOCS"
  printf 'first_code_path=%s\n' "$FIRST_CODE_PATH"
  # scope/correctness/security above are computed on the FULL change and are
  # NEVER recomputed on the delta: a documentation-only delta sitting on top of
  # a code change must still run the security lens.
  printf 'review_mode=%s\n' "$REVIEW_MODE"
  printf 'review_baseline=%s\n' "$BASELINE"
  printf 'delta_file=%s\n' "$([[ "$REVIEW_MODE" == "delta" ]] && printf '%s' "$DELTA_FILE")"
  printf 'changed_files_file=%s\n' "$CHANGED_FILES_FILE"
  printf 'review_scope_reason=%s\n' "$DELTA_REASON"
} > "$SCOPE_TMP"
mv "$SCOPE_TMP" "$SCOPE_FILE"
trap - EXIT

# Human-readable line for the pane, so PO can see the decision without opening
# the scope file.
case "$SCOPE" in
  docs-only)
    printf 'classify-diff: round %s/%s - %s changed paths, all documentation or media: both lenses may be skipped.\n' \
      "$ROUND" "$MAX_ROUNDS" "$TOTAL"
    ;;
  no-changes)
    printf 'classify-diff: round %s/%s - NO changed paths were found; both lenses run, and the empty diff itself needs reporting.\n' \
      "$ROUND" "$MAX_ROUNDS"
    ;;
  *)
    printf 'classify-diff: round %s/%s - %s changed paths, %s of them documentation or media; both lenses run (first non-docs path: %s).\n' \
      "$ROUND" "$MAX_ROUNDS" "$TOTAL" "$DOCS" "$FIRST_CODE_PATH"
    ;;
esac
if [[ "$REVIEW_MODE" == "delta" ]]; then
  printf 'classify-diff: review scope DELTA - %s.\n' "$DELTA_REASON"
else
  printf 'classify-diff: review scope FULL - %s.\n' "$DELTA_REASON"
fi
if [[ "$FINAL_ROUND" == "yes" ]]; then
  printf 'classify-diff: this is the LAST review round allowed by SQUAD_REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS (%s). Report to PO instead of starting another fix round.\n' \
    "$MAX_ROUNDS"
fi
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/classify-diff"

  # capture-review-baseline records the working state a review just finished
  # looking at, so the NEXT round can review only what changed since. One Advisor
  # lens run costs roughly 360k input tokens, almost none of it the task text -
  # it is the lens reading the whole diff and exploring the repo from scratch,
  # every round. Only round 1 needs to do that.
  #
  # `git stash create` is the mechanism. It writes a commit object for the
  # current working state and prints its sha, WITHOUT touching the working tree,
  # the stash list, or what the index actually holds. This runs in the middle of
  # live agent work, so corrupting a Coder's in-progress edit would be far worse
  # than any token saving, and there are tests for all of it.
  #
  # Measured caveat, stated because "does not touch the index" is too strong a
  # claim: git DOES rewrite the .git/index FILE, refreshing its cached stat
  # data. The index CONTENT is untouched - `git ls-files -s`, `git diff` and
  # `git diff --cached` are all byte-identical before and after, which is what
  # the test asserts. Nothing staged is added, removed or altered.
  #
  # It is deliberately run AFTER a review completes, not before. If it ran inside
  # classify-diff, a Manager that re-ran classify-diff without reviewing would
  # advance the baseline past work no lens ever read, and the next delta would
  # SHRINK to exclude it. Capturing afterwards inverts every failure mode: a
  # forgotten or failed capture leaves the OLD baseline (next delta is bigger) or
  # no baseline at all (next review is full). Both directions review more, never
  # less.
  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/capture-review-baseline" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

if [[ $# -ne 3 ]]; then
  printf 'Usage: capture-review-baseline <workdir> <baseline-file> <round>\n' >&2
  exit 2
fi

WORKDIR="$1"
BASELINE_FILE="$2"
ROUND="$3"

[[ -d "$WORKDIR" ]] || {
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: ERROR: not a directory: %s\n' "$WORKDIR" >&2
  exit 2
}
[[ "$ROUND" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: ERROR: round must be a positive integer: %s\n' "$ROUND" >&2
  exit 2
}

# Clear first. A baseline that could not be captured must leave NO file behind:
# classify-diff reads an absent baseline as "review the full diff", which is the
# safe answer. A stale sha from an earlier round would instead produce a delta
# that silently omits everything reviewed between then and now.
rm -f "$BASELINE_FILE"

# `git stash create` on a clean tree prints nothing and exits 0. That is not a
# failure, but it is also not a usable baseline, so it is treated the same as
# any other failure: no file, next review is full.
BASELINE=""
if ! BASELINE="$(git -C "$WORKDIR" stash create 2>/dev/null)"; then
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: could not capture a baseline; the next review will read the FULL diff.\n' >&2
  exit 70
fi
BASELINE="${BASELINE//[[:space:]]/}"
if [[ -z "$BASELINE" ]]; then
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: nothing to capture (no tracked modifications); the next review will read the FULL diff.\n' >&2
  exit 70
fi

# Never write a sha that is not actually a commit in this repository.
if ! git -C "$WORKDIR" cat-file -e "${BASELINE}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: ERROR: git stash create returned something that is not a commit: %s\n' "$BASELINE" >&2
  printf 'capture-review-baseline: the next review will read the FULL diff.\n' >&2
  exit 70
fi

BASELINE_TMP="$(mktemp "${BASELINE_FILE}.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$BASELINE_TMP"' EXIT
{
  printf '%s\n' "$BASELINE"
  printf 'round=%s\n' "$ROUND"
} > "$BASELINE_TMP"
mv "$BASELINE_TMP" "$BASELINE_FILE"
trap - EXIT

printf 'capture-review-baseline: round %s reviewed state %s; round %s will review the delta since it.\n' \
  "$ROUND" "$BASELINE" "$((ROUND + 1))"
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/capture-review-baseline"

  # write-lens-result produces the schema-valid result file for a lens that did
  # NOT run this round: skipped by the scope rule, or carried forward from an
  # earlier clean pass. It exists so the Manager can never hand-write one - a
  # hand-written pass is exactly the silent pass merge-reviews is built to
  # refuse - and so the "carried forward" claim is CHECKED against the previous
  # result rather than trusted.
  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/write-lens-result" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

if [[ $# -ne 6 ]]; then
  printf 'Usage: write-lens-result <lens> skipped <result-file> <status-file> <round> <reason>\n' >&2
  printf '       write-lens-result <lens> carried <result-file> <status-file> <round> <from-round>\n' >&2
  exit 2
fi

LENS="$1"
KIND="$2"
RESULT_FILE="$3"
STATUS_FILE="$4"
ROUND="$5"
DETAIL="$6"

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: jq is required.\n' >&2
  exit 2
}

case "$LENS" in
  correctness|security) ;;
  *)
    printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: lens must be correctness or security: %s\n' "$LENS" >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac

[[ "$ROUND" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
  printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: round must be a positive integer: %s\n' "$ROUND" >&2
  exit 2
}

case "$KIND" in
  skipped)
    [[ -n "$DETAIL" ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: a skipped lens needs a reason.\n' >&2
      exit 2
    }
    jq -n --arg lens "$LENS" --arg round "$ROUND" --arg reason "$DETAIL" '
      {
        verdict: "pass",
        summary: ("SKIPPED: the " + $lens + " lens did not run in review round " + $round + ". Reason: " + $reason),
        must_fix: [],
        should_fix: [],
        test_gaps: []
      }' > "$RESULT_FILE"
    printf 'write-lens-result: %s lens recorded as SKIPPED in round %s (%s).\n' "$LENS" "$ROUND" "$DETAIL"
    ;;
  carried)
    [[ "$DETAIL" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: a carried lens needs the round it is carried FROM: %s\n' "$DETAIL" >&2
      exit 2
    }
    [[ -s "$RESULT_FILE" ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: there is no previous %s result to carry forward: %s\n' "$LENS" "$RESULT_FILE" >&2
      exit 3
    }
    # The previous STATUS, not just the previous result. A lens run can emit
    # pass-shaped JSON and still exit non-zero - the runner died after writing
    # the result, the schema check failed downstream, the pane was killed - and
    # carrying that forward would turn a FAILED lens into a clean pass and let
    # merge-reviews aggregate it as status 0. A non-zero previous status is
    # never carryable; re-run the lens.
    [[ -s "$STATUS_FILE" ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: there is no previous %s status to carry forward: %s\n' "$LENS" "$STATUS_FILE" >&2
      exit 3
    }
    PREVIOUS_STATUS="$(head -n 1 "$STATUS_FILE" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
    # Exactly "0". Anything else - a non-zero code, or a status file that does
    # not hold an exit code at all - is refused rather than interpreted.
    [[ "$PREVIOUS_STATUS" == "0" ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: the previous %s lens run exited [%s], not 0, so it cannot be carried forward. Re-run the lens: %s\n' \
        "$LENS" "$PREVIOUS_STATUS" "$STATUS_FILE" >&2
      exit 3
    }
    # A carry-forward is legal ONLY on top of a clean pass this lens really
    # produced. Re-checked here, never trusted: this is what stops "carried
    # forward" from inventing a pass that never happened, and it refuses to
    # carry a SKIP forward, which would be a review nobody ever ran.
    jq -s -e '
      length == 1
      and (.[0] | type == "object")
      and (.[0] | keys == ["must_fix","should_fix","summary","test_gaps","verdict"])
      and (.[0].verdict == "pass")
      and ((.[0].must_fix | length) == 0)
      and ((.[0].summary | startswith("SKIPPED:")) | not)
    ' "$RESULT_FILE" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: the previous %s result is not a clean pass this lens produced, so it cannot be carried forward. Re-run the lens: %s\n' \
        "$LENS" "$RESULT_FILE" >&2
      exit 3
    }
    CARRIED_TMP="$(mktemp "${RESULT_FILE}.carried.XXXXXX")"
    trap 'rm -f "$CARRIED_TMP"' EXIT
    # An earlier carry marker is stripped before the new one is added, so a lens
    # carried twice says which round it was LAST reviewed in, not a chain.
    jq -s --arg from "$DETAIL" --arg round "$ROUND" '
      .[0]
      | .summary = (.summary | sub("^CARRIED FORWARD from round [0-9]+ into round [0-9]+: "; ""))
      | .summary = ("CARRIED FORWARD from round " + $from + " into round " + $round + ": " + .summary)
    ' "$RESULT_FILE" > "$CARRIED_TMP"
    [[ -s "$CARRIED_TMP" ]] || {
      printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: could not rewrite the %s result.\n' "$LENS" >&2
      exit 3
    }
    mv "$CARRIED_TMP" "$RESULT_FILE"
    trap - EXIT
    printf 'write-lens-result: %s lens CARRIED FORWARD from round %s into round %s.\n' "$LENS" "$DETAIL" "$ROUND"
    ;;
  *)
    printf 'write-lens-result: ERROR: kind must be skipped or carried: %s\n' "$KIND" >&2
    exit 2
    ;;
esac

printf '0\n' > "$STATUS_FILE"
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/write-lens-result"

  # merge-reviews folds the two independent lens results into the single
  # review-result.txt / review-status.txt pair that squad-cockpit reads. The GUI
  # lives in another repository and cannot be changed from here, so those two
  # files MUST keep existing and keep conforming to review-schema.json.
  #
  # The Manager still gates on the two lens files directly. This aggregate is
  # for the GUI, and it is deterministic on purpose: no agent, no tmux event, no
  # run-wait - the Manager runs it synchronously in its own pane.
  #
  # A lens that was SKIPPED by the scope rule or CARRIED FORWARD from an earlier
  # clean pass arrives here as an ordinary schema-valid result, because
  # review-schema.json has no room for a new key and the GUI would reject one.
  # What keeps it from reading like a fresh pass is the marker write-lens-result
  # stamps into the lens summary, which this helper detects and states in the
  # aggregate summary. That marker is the ONLY channel for it - do not add a
  # sixth top-level key.
  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/merge-reviews" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

if [[ $# -ne 6 ]]; then
  printf 'Usage: merge-reviews <correctness-result> <correctness-status> <security-result> <security-status> <out-result> <out-status>\n' >&2
  exit 2
fi

CORRECTNESS_RESULT="$1"
CORRECTNESS_STATUS="$2"
SECURITY_RESULT="$3"
SECURITY_STATUS="$4"
OUT_RESULT="$5"
OUT_STATUS="$6"

# Anything other than a clean merge is reported as this status, and as a
# must_fix verdict. A missing or malformed lens result must never be able to
# reach the GUI as a pass.
MERGE_FAILURE_STATUS=65

# Last-resort net: an unexpected failure (a missing jq, a full disk) still
# leaves a non-zero status behind rather than a stale one from the last round.
trap 'rc=$?; trap - EXIT; if [[ "$rc" -ne 0 && ! -s "$OUT_STATUS" ]]; then printf "%s\n" "$rc" > "$OUT_STATUS"; fi; exit "$rc"' EXIT

# Clear both outputs first. If this helper dies before writing them, absent
# files are the honest answer; the previous round's pass is not.
rm -f "$OUT_RESULT" "$OUT_STATUS"

fail() {
  local message="$1"
  printf 'merge-reviews: ERROR: %s\n' "$message" >&2
  printf 'merge-reviews: refusing to emit a pass. Re-run the failed review lens.\n' >&2
  # Still write a schema-conforming result, so a GUI reading review-result.txt
  # sees must_fix and the reason rather than nothing or something stale.
  jq -n --arg message "$message" '
    {
      verdict: "must_fix",
      summary: ("merge-reviews failed: " + $message),
      must_fix: [("[merge-reviews] " + $message)],
      should_fix: [],
      test_gaps: []
    }' > "$OUT_RESULT" 2>/dev/null || printf '\n' > "$OUT_RESULT"
  printf '%s\n' "$MERGE_FAILURE_STATUS" > "$OUT_STATUS"
  exit "$MERGE_FAILURE_STATUS"
}

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  printf 'merge-reviews: ERROR: jq is required.\n' >&2
  printf '%s\n' "$MERGE_FAILURE_STATUS" > "$OUT_STATUS"
  exit "$MERGE_FAILURE_STATUS"
}

read_status() {
  local lens="$1" file="$2" value
  [[ -s "$file" ]] || fail "the $lens status file is missing or empty: $file"
  value="$(head -n 1 "$file" | tr -d '[:space:]')"
  [[ "$value" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || fail "the $lens status file does not contain an exit code: $file"
  printf '%s\n' "$value"
}

# Full review-schema.json conformance, not just "the fields I am about to read
# exist". The schema sets additionalProperties:false, so an UNEXPECTED top-level
# key is off-schema even when every required key is present and well typed - and
# a lens result carrying stray metadata must not be merged into a pass.
check_result() {
  local lens="$1" file="$2" count extra
  [[ -s "$file" ]] || fail "the $lens result file is missing or empty: $file"

  # EXACTLY ONE JSON value, checked FIRST and enforced for every check below.
  #
  # jq on a multi-document stream runs the filter once per document, and `jq -e`
  # reports the exit status of the LAST one - while the merge reads only the
  # FIRST value. Without this, a bad first object followed by a good second
  # object validates clean and then gets merged, which is the silent pass this
  # whole function exists to prevent. `jq -s` is what makes every later check
  # speak about the same single value the merge will use.
  count="$(jq -s 'length' "$file" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [[ "$count" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] ||
    fail "the $lens result file is not valid JSON: $file"
  [[ "$count" -eq 1 ]] ||
    fail "the $lens result file must contain exactly one JSON value, found $count: $file"

  jq -s -e '.[0] | type == "object"' "$file" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    fail "the $lens result file is not a JSON object: $file"
  extra="$(
    jq -s -r '.[0] | (keys) - ["verdict","summary","must_fix","should_fix","test_gaps"] | join(", ")' \
      "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
  )"
  [[ -z "$extra" ]] ||
    fail "the $lens result file has top-level properties review-schema.json does not allow ($extra): $file"
  jq -s -e '
    .[0] |
    ((keys) == ["must_fix","should_fix","summary","test_gaps","verdict"])
    and (.verdict == "pass" or .verdict == "must_fix")
    and (.summary | type == "string")
    and (.must_fix | type == "array")
    and (.should_fix | type == "array")
    and (.test_gaps | type == "array")
    and ([.must_fix[], .should_fix[], .test_gaps[]] | all(type == "string"))
  ' "$file" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    fail "the $lens result file is not a valid review-schema.json object (missing key, wrong type, or non-string finding): $file"

  # SHAPE is not the whole contract. A result carrying blocking findings while
  # calling itself a pass is internally inconsistent, and it used to merge into
  # an aggregate whose top-level verdict said "pass" while its must_fix array
  # held the finding - which is what the GUI reads. Caught here, where it
  # enters, as well as in the verdict arithmetic below.
  #
  # Only this direction is refused. The reverse - verdict must_fix with an empty
  # must_fix array - is a lens being conservative, it already blocks, and
  # failing the whole merge over it would turn caution into an error.
  jq -s -e '.[0] | (.verdict == "pass" and ((.must_fix | length) > 0)) | not' \
    "$file" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    fail "the $lens result file says verdict \"pass\" but carries must_fix findings, which is not a pass: $file"
}

CORRECTNESS_RC="$(read_status correctness "$CORRECTNESS_STATUS")"
SECURITY_RC="$(read_status security "$SECURITY_STATUS")"
check_result correctness "$CORRECTNESS_RESULT"
check_result security "$SECURITY_RESULT"

# 0 only when BOTH lenses succeeded; otherwise the first non-zero status,
# correctness first.
if [[ "$CORRECTNESS_RC" -ne 0 ]]; then
  AGGREGATE_RC="$CORRECTNESS_RC"
elif [[ "$SECURITY_RC" -ne 0 ]]; then
  AGGREGATE_RC="$SECURITY_RC"
else
  AGGREGATE_RC=0
fi

# must_fix if EITHER lens says must_fix; the arrays are the union of both, each
# finding tagged with the lens that found it.
#
# A lens STATUS is part of the verdict, not just of the aggregate exit code. A
# lens can write pass-shaped JSON and still exit non-zero - the runner died
# after writing the result, the pane went away, the schema check failed
# downstream - and reading only the two JSON verdicts published `verdict: pass`
# for a lens run that never completed. Anything keying off this file, the squad
# GUI included, saw a clean pass. A non-zero status now forces must_fix, adds a
# finding naming the lens and its status, and opens the summary by saying the
# run FAILED rather than implying it reviewed and approved anything. No new
# schema key: the five keys and additionalProperties:false are a contract.
#
# A lens that did not run fresh this round is named in the summary too, so a
# skipped or carried lens can never be mistaken for an ordinary pass either.
# Both banner lines are omitted when both lenses ran fresh and clean, which
# keeps the ordinary aggregate byte-for-byte what it always was.
jq -n \
  --slurpfile correctness "$CORRECTNESS_RESULT" \
  --slurpfile security "$SECURITY_RESULT" \
  --argjson correctness_rc "$CORRECTNESS_RC" \
  --argjson security_rc "$SECURITY_RC" '
  ($correctness[0]) as $c |
  ($security[0]) as $s |
  def lens_state:
    if startswith("SKIPPED:") then "SKIPPED"
    elif test("^CARRIED FORWARD from round [0-9]+ into round [0-9]+:") then
      "CARRIED FORWARD from round " +
        (capture("^CARRIED FORWARD from round (?<from>[0-9]+) into round").from)
    else "FRESH"
    end;
  ($c.summary | lens_state) as $cstate |
  ($s.summary | lens_state) as $sstate |
  (if $cstate == "FRESH" and $sstate == "FRESH" then ""
   else "[lens states] correctness: " + $cstate + "; security: " + $sstate + "\n\n"
   end) as $states |
  ([ (if $correctness_rc != 0 then {lens: "correctness", rc: $correctness_rc} else empty end),
     (if $security_rc != 0 then {lens: "security", rc: $security_rc} else empty end) ]) as $failed |
  ($failed | map("[" + .lens + "] LENS RUN FAILED with status " + (.rc | tostring) +
                 ": this lens did not complete, so it reviewed nothing. Re-run it."))
    as $failure_findings |
  (if ($failed | length) == 0 then ""
   else "[lens run FAILED] " + ($failed | map(.lens + " status " + (.rc | tostring)) | join("; ")) +
        ". The verdict below is must_fix because a lens did not complete, NOT because it" +
        " reviewed and approved anything.\n\n"
   end) as $failure_banner |
  ($failure_findings + ($c.must_fix | map("[correctness] " + .)) + ($s.must_fix | map("[security] " + .)))
    as $all_must_fix |
  {
    # The verdict follows the FINDINGS, not just the two verdict fields. A
    # non-empty must_fix array IS a must_fix aggregate, whatever the lenses
    # called themselves - check_result refuses that inconsistency on the way in,
    # and this refuses to publish it on the way out. The GUI reads this field.
    verdict: (if ($all_must_fix | length) > 0 or ($failed | length) > 0
                 or $c.verdict == "must_fix" or $s.verdict == "must_fix"
              then "must_fix" else "pass" end),
    summary: ($failure_banner + $states + "[correctness] " + $c.summary + "\n\n[security] " + $s.summary),
    must_fix: $all_must_fix,
    should_fix: (($c.should_fix | map("[correctness] " + .)) + ($s.should_fix | map("[security] " + .))),
    test_gaps: (($c.test_gaps | map("[correctness] " + .)) + ($s.test_gaps | map("[security] " + .)))
  }' > "$OUT_RESULT" || fail "could not merge the two lens results"

# Last line of defence, outside jq: the aggregate this helper is about to hand
# the GUI must never say "pass" while carrying blocking findings.
if [[ "$(jq -r '.verdict' "$OUT_RESULT" 2>/dev/null)" == "pass" ]] &&
   [[ "$(jq -r '.must_fix | length' "$OUT_RESULT" 2>/dev/null)" != "0" ]]; then
  fail "refusing to publish a pass aggregate that carries must_fix findings"
fi

[[ -s "$OUT_RESULT" ]] || fail "the merged result came out empty"

printf '%s\n' "$AGGREGATE_RC" > "$OUT_STATUS"
printf 'merge-reviews: correctness status %s, security status %s -> aggregate status %s\n' \
  "$CORRECTNESS_RC" "$SECURITY_RC" "$AGGREGATE_RC"
printf 'merge-reviews: aggregate verdict %s\n' "$(jq -r '.verdict' "$OUT_RESULT")"
LENS_STATES="$(
  jq -r '.summary | split("\n")[0] | select(startswith("[lens states]"))' "$OUT_RESULT" 2>/dev/null || true
)"
[[ -z "$LENS_STATES" ]] || printf 'merge-reviews: %s\n' "$LENS_STATES"
# Exit 0 because the MERGE succeeded. The lens outcome lives in the status file,
# never in this helper's exit code.
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/merge-reviews"

  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/run-rubocop" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
COMMAND_FILE="$1"
RESULT_FILE="$2"
STATUS_FILE="$3"
EVENT="$4"
TITLE_FILE="$5"

log() {
  printf '[%s] %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')" "$*"
}

set_title() {
  # -t "$TMUX_PANE" is required. Without a target tmux retitles the window's
  # ACTIVE pane, which during a real squad is the Manager pane - so the Manager
  # pane would show this agent's status and lose the squad title, while this pane
  # kept a stale label. TMUX_PANE is always set inside a pane.
  if [[ -n "${TMUX_PANE:-}" ]]; then
    tmux select-pane -t "$TMUX_PANE" -T "Coder: $1"
  else
    tmux select-pane -T "Coder: $1"
  fi
}

finish() {
  rc=$?
  trap - EXIT
  printf '%s\n' "$rc" > "$STATUS_FILE"
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]]; then
    set_title "RuboCop passed $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  else
    set_title "RuboCop failed($rc) $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  fi
  tmux wait-for -S "$EVENT"
  exit "$rc"
}
trap finish EXIT

# Clear the previous round's status so its presence unambiguously means "THIS
# round finished". run-wait treats the status file as the single source of
# truth, and the Manager re-runs the Coder via resume and re-runs RuboCop after
# every fix, so a status left over from an earlier round would otherwise let the
# next wait report a stale result as if it were fresh. Done AFTER the trap is
# installed, so a failure from here on still records a status.
rm -f "$STATUS_FILE"

set_title "RuboCop since $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
log ">>> RuboCop starting..."

BASE_COMMAND="$(cat "$COMMAND_FILE")"
if [[ -z "$BASE_COMMAND" ]]; then
  {
    log ">>> RuboCop skipped: not a Ruby project (no RuboCop command configured)."
    printf 'RuboCop skipped: not a Ruby project.\n'
  } | tee "$RESULT_FILE"
  exit 0
fi

RUBOCOP_FILES=()
while IFS= read -r file; do
  case "$file" in
    app/*|config/*|db/*|lib/*|spec/*|test/*|Gemfile|Rakefile|config.ru|*.rb|*.rake|*.gemspec|*.jbuilder|*.builder)
      [[ -f "$file" ]] && RUBOCOP_FILES+=("$file")
      ;;
  esac
done < <(
  {
    # HEAD does not exist in a repository without commits; everything is
    # untracked there and covered by ls-files below.
    if git rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD >/dev/null; then
      git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRT HEAD --
    fi
    git ls-files --others --exclude-standard
  } | sort -u
)

if [[ "${#RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}" -eq 0 ]]; then
  {
    log ">>> RuboCop skipped: no changed Rails/Ruby files."
    printf 'No changed Rails/Ruby files for RuboCop.\n'
  } | tee "$RESULT_FILE"
  exit 0
fi

{
  printf 'RuboCop target files:\n'
  printf '%s\n' "${RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}"
} > "$RESULT_FILE"
log ">>> RuboCop target files:"
printf '%s\n' "${RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}" |
  while IFS= read -r line; do log "  $line"; done

printf '%q\n' "${RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}" > "${RESULT_FILE%.txt}.files"
AUTOCORRECT_LOG="${RESULT_FILE%.txt}.autocorrect.log"

log ">>> RuboCop autocorrect starting..."
{
  printf '\nRuboCop autocorrect (-a):\n'
  set +e
  bash -lc "$BASE_COMMAND -a --cache true \"\$@\"" bash "${RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}"
  autocorrect_status=$?
  set -e
  printf 'RuboCop autocorrect exit status: %s\n' "$autocorrect_status"
} > "$AUTOCORRECT_LOG" 2>&1
cat "$AUTOCORRECT_LOG" >> "$RESULT_FILE"
while IFS= read -r line; do log "$line"; done < "$AUTOCORRECT_LOG"

log ">>> RuboCop verification starting..."
bash -lc "$BASE_COMMAND --cache true \"\$@\"" bash "${RUBOCOP_FILES[@]}" 2>&1 |
  tee -a "$RESULT_FILE" |
  while IFS= read -r line; do log "$line"; done
log ">>> RuboCop autocorrect and verification finished for changed Rails/Ruby files."
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/run-rubocop"

  # Brakeman is a GATE, not a review lens: it does not feed merge-reviews and it
  # has no verdict to merge. It either passes with zero warnings, is skipped
  # because the project has no Brakeman, or it fails the round.
  #
  # WHOLE-APP scan, deliberately. Brakeman is a dataflow analyser: a warning
  # routinely originates in a controller, view or route the change never
  # touched, because the change made an existing path reachable. Filtering to
  # changed files would produce confident silence in exactly the case this gate
  # exists to catch.
  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/run-brakeman" <<'HELPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
COMMAND_FILE="$1"
RESULT_FILE="$2"
STATUS_FILE="$3"
EVENT="$4"
TITLE_FILE="$5"

JSON_REPORT="${RESULT_FILE%.txt}.json"
CONSOLE_LOG="${RESULT_FILE%.txt}.console.log"
# RELATIVE on purpose. Under the docker-compose branch the scan runs inside the
# container, where $SQUAD_DIR is not mounted but the worktree is - so an
# absolute host path for -o would fail there and nowhere else. A relative path
# resolves against the app directory in both cases. It is moved into $SQUAD_DIR
# and removed from the worktree before this helper exits, so it can never show
# up as an uncommitted change in the review that follows.
REPORT_BASENAME=".squad-brakeman-report.json"

log() {
  printf '[%s] %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')" "$*"
}

set_title() {
  # See run-rubocop: without -t this retitles the active (Manager) pane.
  if [[ -n "${TMUX_PANE:-}" ]]; then
    tmux select-pane -t "$TMUX_PANE" -T "Coder: $1"
  else
    tmux select-pane -T "Coder: $1"
  fi
}

finish() {
  rc=$?
  trap - EXIT
  rm -f "$REPORT_BASENAME"
  printf '%s\n' "$rc" > "$STATUS_FILE"
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]]; then
    set_title "Brakeman passed $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  else
    set_title "Brakeman failed($rc) $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
  fi
  tmux wait-for -S "$EVENT"
  exit "$rc"
}
trap finish EXIT

# Same stale-status protection as every other helper: cleared AFTER the trap is
# installed, so a failure from here on still records a status.
rm -f "$STATUS_FILE"

set_title "Brakeman since $(date '+%H:%M:%S')"
log ">>> Brakeman starting..."

skip() {
  {
    log ">>> Brakeman skipped: $1"
    printf 'Brakeman skipped: %s\n' "$1"
  } | tee "$RESULT_FILE"
  exit 0
}

fail() {
  {
    log ">>> Brakeman FAILED: $1"
    printf 'Brakeman failed: %s\n' "$1"
  } | tee -a "$RESULT_FILE"
  exit "${2:-1}"
}

BASE_COMMAND="$(cat "$COMMAND_FILE")"
if [[ -z "$BASE_COMMAND" ]]; then
  skip "no Brakeman command configured (not a Ruby project, or Brakeman is not installed)."
fi

# Proof that BRAKEMAN ITSELF is absent - not that something else went wrong.
#
# Every pattern must NAME brakeman on the SAME LINE as an "this executable does
# not exist" phrase. That pairing is the entire point. A bare "Could not find
# ..." naming some other gem means the BUNDLE is broken, and a broken toolchain
# has to FAIL this gate, not quietly switch it off.
#
# When a failure cannot be classified, it is a FAILURE. The asymmetry is not
# close: a false failure costs one confused round and is immediately visible,
# while a false skip hides every security warning in the change set and still
# reports green.
#
# Deliberately NOT treated as proof of absence:
#   * "Could not find <gem>-<version> in locally installed gems", even when the
#     gem named IS brakeman. Detection already ran brakeman successfully at
#     launch, so that message means the environment regressed afterwards - and a
#     project that declares brakeman must not have its security gate disabled by
#     a stale bundle.
#   * Any docker daemon or container error.
#   * Any unrecognised output.
brakeman_absent() {
  printf '%s' "$1" | grep -qiE \
    -e 'command not found:[[:space:]]*brakeman' \
    -e 'brakeman:[[:space:]]*command not found' \
    -e 'cannot load such file --[[:space:]]*brakeman' \
    -e 'brakeman.*no such file or directory' \
    -e 'no such file or directory.*brakeman'
}

# Probe before scanning, so "brakeman is not installed here" is distinguishable
# from "the scan failed". ONLY the signatures above may become a skip: a docker
# daemon that is not running, a broken bundle, or any other failure is a real
# failure and must stop the round.
log ">>> Verifying Brakeman is runnable: $BASE_COMMAND --version"
set +e
VERSION_OUTPUT="$(bash -lc "$BASE_COMMAND --version" 2>&1)"
VERSION_STATUS=$?
set -e
if [[ "$VERSION_STATUS" -ne 0 ]]; then
  printf '%s\n' "$VERSION_OUTPUT" | while IFS= read -r line; do log "  $line"; done
  if brakeman_absent "$VERSION_OUTPUT"; then
    skip "Brakeman itself is not installed for this project (probe exit $VERSION_STATUS)."
  fi
  {
    printf 'Brakeman probe command: %s --version\n' "$BASE_COMMAND"
    printf 'Brakeman probe exit status: %s\n' "$VERSION_STATUS"
    printf '%s\n' "$VERSION_OUTPUT"
  } > "$RESULT_FILE"
  fail "the Brakeman probe failed for a reason that does NOT prove Brakeman is absent - a broken bundle, a docker or runtime failure, or an unrecognised error (see $RESULT_FILE). This is NOT treated as a skip." "$VERSION_STATUS"
fi
log ">>> Brakeman version: $(printf '%s' "$VERSION_OUTPUT" | head -n 1)"

rm -f "$REPORT_BASENAME" "$JSON_REPORT"

# -q            no informational banner
# --no-pager    never page; this is non-interactive
# --exit-on-warn  a warning is a non-zero exit, which is the whole point
# -o <file>     JSON report (format is taken from the .json extension)
# .             the WHOLE application, not a file list
log ">>> Brakeman scanning the whole application..."
set +e
bash -lc "$BASE_COMMAND -q --no-pager --exit-on-warn -o \"\$1\" ." bash "$REPORT_BASENAME" \
  > "$CONSOLE_LOG" 2>&1
SCAN_STATUS=$?
set -e
while IFS= read -r line; do log "$line"; done < "$CONSOLE_LOG"

if [[ -f "$REPORT_BASENAME" ]]; then
  mv "$REPORT_BASENAME" "$JSON_REPORT"
fi

{
  printf 'Brakeman command: %s\n' "$BASE_COMMAND"
  printf 'Brakeman exit status: %s\n' "$SCAN_STATUS"
  printf '\nBrakeman console output:\n'
  cat "$CONSOLE_LOG"
} > "$RESULT_FILE"

# Summarise the report BEFORE deciding pass or fail. The failing path is exactly
# where the warning list matters - it is what the Manager quotes to the Coder -
# so it must not be skipped by an early exit.
WARNING_COUNT=""
ERROR_COUNT=""
REPORT_USABLE=false
if [[ -s "$JSON_REPORT" ]] && command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  if jq -e 'type == "object" and (.warnings | type == "array")' "$JSON_REPORT" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    REPORT_USABLE=true
    WARNING_COUNT="$(jq -r '.warnings | length' "$JSON_REPORT")"
    ERROR_COUNT="$(jq -r '(.errors // []) | length' "$JSON_REPORT")"
    {
      printf '\nBrakeman summary: %s warnings, %s scan errors.\n' "$WARNING_COUNT" "$ERROR_COUNT"
      if [[ "$WARNING_COUNT" -gt 0 ]]; then
        jq -r '.warnings[] | "- [\(.confidence // "?")] \(.warning_type // "?"): \(.message // "") (\(.file // "?"):\(.line // "?"))"' \
          "$JSON_REPORT"
      fi
    } >> "$RESULT_FILE"
    log ">>> Brakeman summary: $WARNING_COUNT warnings, $ERROR_COUNT scan errors."
  fi
fi

# Exit 4 means "no Rails application detected". Trusting the NUMBER alone would
# turn any future meaning of 4 into a silent skip, so the output has to say so
# too. Every other non-zero status is a failure.
if [[ "$SCAN_STATUS" -eq 4 ]]; then
  if grep -qiE 'please supply the path to a rails application|no rails application|not a rails application' \
    "$CONSOLE_LOG"; then
    skip "no Rails application was detected in this repository (Brakeman exit 4)."
  fi
  fail "Brakeman exited 4 but did not report a missing Rails application, so this is not a skip." "$SCAN_STATUS"
fi

if [[ "$SCAN_STATUS" -ne 0 ]]; then
  fail "Brakeman exited $SCAN_STATUS. With --exit-on-warn this means warnings were found, or the scan itself errored. Read $RESULT_FILE and $JSON_REPORT." "$SCAN_STATUS"
fi

# A zero exit is not sufficient on its own: if the JSON report is missing or
# unreadable there is nothing proving the scan actually happened, and reporting
# a pass from an absent report is the failure mode this gate cannot have.
if [[ ! -s "$JSON_REPORT" ]]; then
  fail "Brakeman exited 0 but wrote no JSON report to $JSON_REPORT, so the scan cannot be confirmed." 1
fi

if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  if [[ "$REPORT_USABLE" != "true" ]]; then
    fail "the Brakeman JSON report at $JSON_REPORT is malformed or has no warnings array." 1
  fi
  # Belt and braces: --exit-on-warn should already have produced a non-zero
  # status, so reaching here with warnings means the flag did not do what it
  # claims. Fail rather than trust the exit code alone.
  if [[ "$WARNING_COUNT" -gt 0 ]]; then
    fail "Brakeman exited 0 but the report contains $WARNING_COUNT warnings." 1
  fi
  if [[ "$ERROR_COUNT" -gt 0 ]]; then
    fail "Brakeman reported $ERROR_COUNT scan errors, so the scan is not trustworthy." 1
  fi
else
  printf '\n(jq is unavailable, so no summary was extracted from %s)\n' "$JSON_REPORT" >> "$RESULT_FILE"
fi

log ">>> Brakeman passed with 0 warnings (whole-application scan)."
HELPER
  chmod +x "$SQUAD_DIR/run-brakeman"
}

# --- Manager system prompt ----------------------------------------------------
# Contains NO tmux pane ID: every agent is addressed by role, so the prompt stays
# valid across any number of resumes. Regenerated on resume for the same reason.
generate_manager_prompt() {
  local plan_event="squad-$RUN_ID-plan"
  local coder_event="squad-$RUN_ID-coder"
  local rubocop_event="squad-$RUN_ID-rubocop"
  local brakeman_event="squad-$RUN_ID-brakeman"
  # One event per review lens. The two lenses run one after the other in the
  # same pane, and reusing a single event name for both would let the second
  # run-wait return on the first lens's signal.
  local review_correctness_event="squad-$RUN_ID-review-correctness"
  local review_security_event="squad-$RUN_ID-review-security"

  set_worktree_mode

  cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/manager-prompt.md" << PROMPT
# You are a Development Manager

You coordinate a Coder and an Advisor in separate tmux panes.
You do NOT write code yourself.
Speak Japanese to PO (user). Write English for agents.

## Environment

- Original worktree: $REPO_ROOT
- Squad worktree: $WORKDIR
- Worktree mode: $WORKTREE_MODE
- Squad branch: $BRANCH
- Squad title: $SQUAD_TITLE
- Detected ticket ID: ${TICKET_ID:-not detected}
- Artifacts: $SQUAD_DIR
- Manager session ID: $MANAGER_SESSION_ID
- Coder session ID: $CODER_SESSION_ID
- Wait timeout: $WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS seconds
- Review round cap: $REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS rounds (SQUAD_REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS)

$WORKTREE_NOTE
Agents must not create additional worktrees. In particular, do not create
worktrees under the repository such as .claude/worktree or .claude/worktrees.

## Agent addressing (roles, never pane IDs)

tmux assigns new pane IDs to every pane whenever this squad is resumed, so a
pane ID is NEVER a stable address. Address agents by role only. The roles are
exactly: manager, coder, advisor.

  '$SQUAD_DIR/squad-pane' <role>              # prints the live pane ID for a role
  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' <role> '<command>'     # runs one command in that role's pane

Both resolve the pane from the tmux options tagged on it, require exactly one
match for this run, and exit non-zero (sending nothing) otherwise. run-wait
likewise takes a role, not a pane ID.

- NEVER run 'tmux send-keys' yourself, and never write a literal tmux pane ID
  (a %-prefixed number such as the ones tmux prints) in any command.
- If a helper reports that a role cannot be resolved, report that to PO and stop.
  Do not guess a pane ID.

## Critical rules

- BEFORE asking Advisor to plan, inspect the ticket URL or task context and
  replace the provisional squad title with a specific title.
- A squad title MUST include the ticket ID when one exists, the business object
  or feature area, and the concrete change. Avoid vague titles such as
  "CSV import", "API fix", or "bug fix". For example:
  "EAR-123 | 車両マスタCSVインポートの重複検証追加".
- NEVER run codex or claude commands directly. ALL work MUST go through the
  send-to helper so that it lands in the agent panes.
- NEVER skip the Advisor planning step.
- ALWAYS follow the full workflow in order.
- The PO must be able to see agent work in the tmux panes.
- Use the run-wait helper to wait for completion. NEVER poll with capture-pane.
- NEVER run tmux kill-server, with or without -L or TMUX_TMPDIR. Do not ask
  Coder or Advisor to run it either. If a temporary tmux server is created for
  testing, leave it running and report the cleanup command to PO instead of
  executing it.
- NEVER run tmux kill-session, kill-window, or kill-pane unless PO explicitly
  requests that exact tmux operation. This includes test cleanup commands.
- At the VERY START of every turn, before anything else, run:
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' clear
  It is free when nothing is marked. This is what takes the waiting badge down
  once PO has answered, including when they answered directly in the tmux pane,
  so it must run whether or not you think you were waiting.
- BEFORE asking PO a question, or pausing for PO approval of anything, run:
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' mark '<short reason>'
  Run it BEFORE you write the question, not after: once you have asked, you stop
  and PO may not look at this window for hours. PO is often not watching this
  window - sometimes not even this machine - and the marker is the only thing
  that tells them you are blocked.
- After each Coder run, ALWAYS run the RuboCop helper and require exit status 0.
  The helper runs RuboCop autocorrect and verification only on changed
  Rails/Ruby files, never the full repo.
- After RuboCop and BEFORE the review lenses, ALWAYS run the Brakeman helper and
  require exit status 0. It scans the WHOLE application, not the changed files,
  because Brakeman is a dataflow scanner and a warning often surfaces in code
  this change never touched. It skips with status 0 when the project has no
  Brakeman, so a non-zero status always means a real finding or a real failure.
- Brakeman is a GATE, not a third review lens. Never pass its files to
  merge-reviews and never treat it as a review verdict.
- The Coder MUST run relevant automated tests for the task. If tests cannot run,
  report the exact blocker to PO and do not claim completion.
- RuboCop success is required independently of the Coder's report.
- The review is TWO INDEPENDENT LENSES: correctness first, then security. They
  must stay independent. NEVER include, quote, summarize, or point at the
  correctness result in the security task text, and never the other way round.
  A lens that is told what the other already found stops looking for what the
  other missed, which is the entire reason there are two of them.
- LENS INDEPENDENCE IS ABSOLUTE, and nothing below weakens it. It holds for a
  re-run lens, for a carried-forward lens and for a skipped one alike. Only
  merge-reviews ever sees both results, and it runs AFTERWARDS. The two prompts
  never cross-feed.
- Round 1 reviews the FULL uncommitted diff. Round 2 and later review only what
  changed since the state the previous round reviewed, plus that lens's own
  previous findings. classify-diff decides which, and writes it as review_mode;
  read it, never decide it yourself, and never narrow a full round by hand.
- EVERY failure around the delta falls back to the FULL diff - a missing,
  unreadable or unusable baseline, a delta that cannot be computed, a delta that
  comes back empty. There is no path that reviews less than the whole change
  unless a real delta was computed. When in doubt, the full diff.
- After every review round, run capture-review-baseline. Before a review, never:
  a baseline captured for a review that then does not happen would move the
  starting line past work no lens ever read.
- Round 1, the first review of this run, ALWAYS runs BOTH lenses, subject only
  to the classify-diff skip rule.
- In a later round a lens MUST be re-run when ANY of these hold: it previously
  returned must_fix; its previous status was non-zero or its previous result was
  malformed; it was skipped last round and the diff now makes it in scope; or
  the diff changed at all in a way relevant to that lens. Since Coder edits code
  to fix findings, the DEFAULT is re-run. Carry-forward is the narrow exception,
  allowed ONLY when that lens returned a clean pass AND nothing relevant to it
  has changed since. When in doubt, re-run.
- A lens is never skipped or carried forward by hand. The only ways to record
  one are the classify-diff scope file and the write-lens-result helper.
- After BOTH lens results exist - whether run, carried or skipped - ALWAYS run
  the merge-reviews helper. It writes the aggregate
  $SQUAD_DIR/review-status.txt and $SQUAD_DIR/review-result.txt that the squad
  GUI reads. Your own decisions still come from the two lens files.
- There is a HARD cap of $REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS review rounds.
  When classify-diff reports final_round=yes, this is the last review round: do
  NOT start another Coder fix round after it. Run
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' mark 'review round cap reached'
  and report the outstanding findings, the test results and the RuboCop and
  Brakeman gate results to PO instead.
- NEVER commit, push, force-push, merge, rebase, or create a pull request unless
  PO explicitly asks for that Git operation. Editing files is allowed.
- NEVER create additional git worktrees, including Claude Code default locations
  such as .claude/worktree or .claude/worktrees. Use only the isolated squad
  worktree: $WORKDIR.

## Status handling

Each helper writes an exit code to its status file before signaling completion.
After every run-wait command, read the status file. If it is not 0, read
the corresponding result file if present, report the failure to PO, and decide
whether the Coder can fix it. Never wait indefinitely after a helper failure.
run-wait writes status 125 when the role no longer resolves to a live pane, and
applies a $WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS second timeout as a final fallback.

## Result files

- $SQUAD_DIR/advisor-result.txt   - the implementation plan
- $SQUAD_DIR/coder-result.txt     - the Coder's structured verification report
- $SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-result.txt   - RuboCop autocorrect and verification output
- $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.txt  - Brakeman gate output, with a warning summary
- $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.json - the raw Brakeman JSON report. Both lenses may
  cite it as evidence; the SECURITY lens must read it. It is a GATE result, not
  a review verdict, and it is never an input to merge-reviews.
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-scope.txt     - which lenses this round needs, which round
  it is, and whether this round reviews the full diff or only the delta. Written
  by classify-diff. Read it, never guess it.
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-delta.diff    - in a delta round, what changed since the
  state the previous round reviewed. Written by classify-diff, and absent in a
  full round.
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-changed-files.txt - every file the WHOLE change touches, not
  just the delta. Both lenses get this path in every round.
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-baseline.txt  - the working state the last review read,
  written by capture-review-baseline. Never write it by hand.
- $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.correctness.md - the review lenses for the CORRECTNESS
  review. Referenced by that task file, never pasted into it.
- $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.security.md    - the review lenses for the SECURITY
  review. Referenced by that task file, never pasted into it.
- $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.merged.md  - both lists in one file. It is the audit
  record of what was in force, not an input to a review task.
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-result.txt - correctness lens findings
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-security-result.txt    - security lens findings
- $SQUAD_DIR/review-result.txt    - the AGGREGATE of the two lenses, written by
  merge-reviews. It exists for the squad GUI, which reads this file and
  $SQUAD_DIR/review-status.txt and cannot be changed from here. Never write it
  by hand, and never base a decision on it alone.

EITHER lens may return must_fix, and they see different things: a pass from the
correctness lens says nothing about the security lens. When you ask Coder to
fix, take the UNION of must_fix from BOTH lens result files. Include the
should_fix and test_gaps items from both as well when they are cheap to address,
and say which lens raised each finding.

## Commands

### Update the squad title after reading the ticket

Read the ticket URL or task context first, then write TWO files and run one
command. They are one step - never write one without the other.

1. $SQUAD_DIR/new-title.txt - the long title, shown on the Manager PANE.
   A concise, specific Japanese title. Include the detected ticket ID
   (${TICKET_ID:-none}) when available. Distinguish this squad from parallel
   work on similar features by naming the business object and concrete change.

2. $SQUAD_DIR/new-short-name.txt - the short name, shown as the tmux WINDOW
   name. About two words, at most 20 display columns (roughly ten Japanese
   characters); anything longer is truncated. It must say WHAT THIS RUN IS
   DOING - not the tool, not a timestamp, not the ticket ID on its own.
   Good: 車両CSV重複検証 / 権限まわり修正 / レポートHTML化
   Bad:  squad / 作業中 / EAR-123 / 車両マスタCSVインポートの重複検証を追加する
   It sits in a window list beside other windows, so brevity is the point.

Then run:

  cat > '$SQUAD_DIR/new-title.txt' <<'EOF'
  (specific title, for example: EAR-123 | 車両マスタCSVインポートの重複検証追加)
  EOF
  cat > '$SQUAD_DIR/new-short-name.txt' <<'EOF'
  (two words, for example: CSV重複検証)
  EOF
  '$SQUAD_DIR/update-title' '$SQUAD_DIR/title.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/new-title.txt' '$WINDOW_SHORT_NAME'

Re-run this whole step whenever the work materially changes - when the plan
lands, or when the squad moves on to something the current name no longer
describes. Keep both files in step with each other; the window name going stale
is how PO loses track of which window is which.

### Tell PO you are waiting for them

PO is not necessarily watching this window. Mark the run as waiting whenever you
are about to hand control back to them for an answer or a decision:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' mark 'plan approval'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' clear
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' status

mark adds a marker to the tmux WINDOW name, so PO can see it in the window list
with this squad's window in the background, and it stays visible even if the
squad is later resumed. The reason is one short line - "plan approval",
"which API to use", "needs a production credential".

Both commands are idempotent and safe to repeat. clear at the start of a turn is
mandatory (see Critical rules); it is what lowers the marker after PO replies.
Marking never changes the squad title, and updating the title while marked keeps
the marker.

### Ask Advisor for an implementation plan

Write an English request to $SQUAD_DIR/advisor-task.txt. Ask Advisor to inspect
the repository, produce a scoped implementation plan, define acceptance
criteria, identify relevant tests, and call out risks. Then run:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/advisor-status.txt' advisor '$SQUAD_DIR/run-advisor $SQUAD_DIR/advisor-task.txt $SQUAD_DIR/advisor-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/advisor-status.txt $plan_event $SQUAD_DIR/plan-schema.json $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$plan_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/advisor-status.txt' advisor '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Advisor planning'
