  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/advisor-result.txt'

### Ask Coder to implement

Write an English request to $SQUAD_DIR/coder-task.txt. Include the approved plan
and acceptance criteria. Require Coder to inspect project rules, implement the
change, run relevant automated tests, run RuboCop and Brakeman, and return the
structured verification report. Its rubocop_status and brakeman_status fields
are self-reports: the authoritative results are the helper runs you do yourself
in the steps below. For broad or uncertain changes, require Coder to use
built-in Explore subagents to investigate independent areas in parallel before
editing. Keep all file writes in the main Coder session, and skip subagents for
narrow changes. Explicitly prohibit commit, push, force-push, merge, rebase, and
pull-request creation unless PO requested that specific Git operation. Explicitly
prohibit creating any additional git worktree, including .claude/worktree or
.claude/worktrees. Explicitly prohibit tmux kill-server, tmux kill-session,
tmux kill-window, and tmux kill-pane; tell Coder to leave any temporary tmux
test server running and report the cleanup command instead. Then run:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt' coder '$SQUAD_DIR/run-coder $SQUAD_DIR/coder-task.txt $SQUAD_DIR/coder-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt $coder_event $CODER_SESSION_ID $SQUAD_DIR/coder-schema.json initial $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$coder_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt' coder '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Coder'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-result.txt'

### Run mandatory RuboCop verification

Always run this after Coder implementation and after each fix:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-status.txt' coder '$SQUAD_DIR/run-rubocop $SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-command.txt $SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-status.txt $rubocop_event $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$rubocop_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-status.txt' coder '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'RuboCop'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-result.txt'

RuboCop status must be 0. The helper first runs rubocop -a --cache true on the
changed Rails/Ruby files, then runs verification on the same files. If there are
no changed Rails/Ruby files, or the project is not a Ruby project (no RuboCop
command configured), the helper records that and returns 0. The configured
command uses --fail-level W, so warnings also fail verification.

### Run mandatory Brakeman verification

Always run this after RuboCop and BEFORE the review lenses, and again after each
fix:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-status.txt' coder '$SQUAD_DIR/run-brakeman $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-command.txt $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-status.txt $brakeman_event $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$brakeman_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-status.txt' coder '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Brakeman'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.txt'

Brakeman status must be 0. The scan covers the WHOLE application and requires
ZERO warnings; a project's curated config/brakeman.ignore is what carries any
accepted pre-existing warnings. The helper returns 0 when the project is not a
Ruby project, has no Brakeman installed, or contains no Rails application, so a
non-zero status is always a real finding or a real failure - never a missing
tool. Never work around a failure by narrowing the scan; either fix the warning
or ask PO about the ignore file.

### The output contract (put it FIRST in every review task file)

Both review task files must OPEN with this contract, before the task
description. The end of the file demonstrably does not work: the schema alone
does not reliably stop a lens from answering in prose, and a prose result costs
a full re-run of that lens because merge-reviews refuses to merge it.

Tell Advisor its FINAL message must be a single raw JSON object conforming to
review-schema.json and nothing else - no prose before or after, no markdown code
fences - carrying exactly these five top-level keys and no others: verdict,
summary, must_fix, should_fix, test_gaps. merge-reviews enforces the schema's
additionalProperties:false, so an extra key is as fatal as prose.

A lens that finds NOTHING still answers with the object: verdict "pass" with
empty arrays. A sentence saying the change looks fine is a FAILED lens run, not
a pass. verdict is "must_fix" when the must_fix array is non-empty, otherwise
"pass".

### Classify the diff before the review lenses (mandatory, once per round)

Run this once at the start of EVERY review round, in your own pane, before you
write any review task:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/classify-diff' '$WORKDIR' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-scope.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-round.txt' '$REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-baseline.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-delta.diff' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-changed-files.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-scope.txt'

It writes round, max_rounds, final_round, scope, correctness, security, the
changed-path counts, and the review scope for this round: review_mode,
review_baseline, delta_file, changed_files_file and review_scope_reason.
THE SKIP RULE it applies, in one sentence:

  A review lens is skipped ONLY when every path the change touches - both
  sides of a rename, and deletions too - is either a non-executable media
  file or a non-executable documentation file whose content this classifier
  read in full and found free of active content; anything else - executable
  code and any file git records as executable whatever its name, a document
  carrying a script, an embed, an event-handler attribute, a javascript: or
  data: URL, a leading shebang or an evaluable source block, a document too
  large, binary, deleted or unreadable to vouch for, shell scripts, config,
  dependency and lock files, CI definitions, env samples, database, migration
  and schema files, routes and API definitions, templates and views,
  generated runtime artifacts, symlinks and submodules, and any extension the
  classifier does not recognise - runs it.

Unknown means RUN. A documentation extension is necessary but NOT sufficient:
the classifier reads the file, and a document carrying active content is code.
Never override the helper towards skipping. If classify-diff exits non-zero, the
diff could not be classified: run BOTH lenses and tell PO.

The SKIP decision above is always computed on the FULL change, never on the
delta. A documentation-only delta sitting on top of a code change still runs the
security lens. That is deliberately the expensive direction; do not optimise it.

### What each round REVIEWS (full diff, or only the delta)

One Advisor lens run costs roughly 360k input tokens, almost none of it the task
text - it is the lens reading the whole diff and exploring the repo from
scratch. Only the first round needs to do that. Round 1 is the real review;
later rounds exist to confirm that specific findings were fixed and that the fix
broke nothing.

classify-diff decides this for you and writes it as review_mode. Read it. Never
decide the scope yourself.

  review_mode=full   Review ALL uncommitted changes, exactly as round 1 always
                     does. This is also what you get whenever anything about the
                     delta could not be established.
  review_mode=delta  Review the contents of delta_file: what changed since the
                     state the previous round reviewed.

In a delta round the review task must ALSO carry, for every lens:

- The path in changed_files_file. It lists every file the WHOLE change touches,
  not just the delta, and it is a few hundred bytes. It is what lets a lens
  notice that the delta touches something it should go and read more of.
- The must_fix findings THAT SAME LENS raised last round, which the Coder was
  asked to fix. Never the other lens's findings - see the independence rule.
- This instruction, in the task text, in these words: the delta is the PRIMARY
  SCOPE, not a blindfold. Tell Advisor it MAY and SHOULD read the surrounding
  files, the earlier change and any history it needs whenever the delta does not
  make sense on its own, and that a fix which looks correct in isolation but
  breaks its caller is exactly what this round is for.

Every fallback goes towards reading MORE, never less. If review_mode is full,
read the full diff and do not go looking for a delta. If classify-diff exits
non-zero at all, run BOTH lenses on the full diff and tell PO.

### Capture the review baseline (mandatory, after every review round)

Run this in your own pane once the round's review is finished - after
merge-reviews, whether the verdict was pass or must_fix:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/capture-review-baseline' '$WORKDIR' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-baseline.txt' '<round>'

It records the working state this round just reviewed, so the next round can
review only what changed since. It uses \`git stash create\`, which writes a
commit object WITHOUT touching the working tree, the stash list, or anything
staged in the index - it is safe to run while Coder has work in progress.

Run it AFTER the review, never before. A baseline captured before a review that
then does not happen would move the starting line past work no lens ever read.
If it fails, or you forget it, the next round simply reads the full diff again -
that costs tokens and is safe. Never hand-write this file.

### The review checklists (one file per lens)

Each lens has its own derived checklist file:

  $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.correctness.md   for the CORRECTNESS review
  $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.security.md      for the SECURITY review

Do NOT paste either file into a task file, and do NOT read them yourself.
Instead, each review task file carries a "Files to read before reviewing"
section naming its own lens file by absolute path, and instructs Advisor to read
that file FIRST and to use it as the lenses for the review. Referencing costs
one read by one agent; pasting costs the whole list in every prompt of every
round, which is what this is here to avoid.

Never name the other lens's checklist file, and never name checklist.merged.md,
in a review task: the merged file is the audit record, not a review input.

They are lists of LENSES to look through, not boxes to tick. Tell Advisor to
report only what it finds wrong - no coverage table, no "this lens was fine".
review-schema.json findings are flat strings, so tell Advisor to put the
checklist ID at the FRONT of the finding text, for example:

  [SEC-001] Missing tenant boundary check in app/controllers/reports_controller.rb

If a lens file says "(no checklist items)", that lens has no checklist for this
project. Say so in that task text and let that lens run unstructured; that is
not an error and never a reason to skip a lens.

### Ask Advisor for a CORRECTNESS review

Run this when review-scope.txt says correctness=run and you are not carrying the
lens forward. The first of two independent lenses. Write an English request to
$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-task.txt. Tell Advisor to inspect the change
against the approved acceptance criteria, the Coder's test report, and the
mandatory RuboCop and Brakeman results, and to cover correctness, data
integrity, tests, compatibility, performance, and the project's own conventions.
Report findings only. A pass verdict is allowed only when relevant tests ran and
both the RuboCop and Brakeman statuses are 0. The lens may cite the Brakeman
result as evidence.

Scope the task from review_mode, as described above:

- review_mode=full - all uncommitted changes.
- review_mode=delta - the contents of delta_file, PLUS the CORRECTNESS must_fix
  findings from last round, PLUS the changed_files_file path. Say in the task
  text that the delta is the primary scope and not a blindfold: Advisor may and
  should read the surrounding files and the earlier change whenever the delta
  does not make sense alone.

The task file must carry a "Files to read before reviewing" section, and that
section must name $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.correctness.md as a file Advisor MUST
read before reviewing. Reading it is not optional and it is not summarised for
Advisor by you. In a delta round that section also names delta_file and
changed_files_file. Never name checklist.security.md or checklist.merged.md
here, and never the security lens's findings.

Open this task file with the output contract above; the contract stays FIRST in
the file, ahead of the files-to-read section and everything else. Then run:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt' advisor '$SQUAD_DIR/run-review $SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-task.txt $SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt $review_correctness_event $SQUAD_DIR/review-schema.json $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt correctness'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$review_correctness_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt' advisor '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Advisor correctness review'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-result.txt'

### Ask Advisor for a SECURITY review

Run this when review-scope.txt says security=run and you are not carrying the
lens forward. It runs even when the correctness lens returned must_fix, and its
outcome never depends on the correctness lens's. Write an English request to
$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-task.txt. Tell Advisor to inspect the same change
INDEPENDENTLY for authorization, tenant and user isolation, input validation,
injection, information disclosure, secret handling, and filesystem, shell and
database risks. Report findings only.

Scope the task from review_mode exactly as the correctness lens does, from the
SAME delta_file and the SAME changed_files_file - but with the SECURITY must_fix
findings from last round, never the correctness ones. In a delta round say in
the task text that the delta is the primary scope and not a blindfold: Advisor
may and should read the surrounding files and the earlier change whenever the
delta does not make sense alone.

Tell it to READ $SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.txt and, when present,
$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.json as evidence - a passing Brakeman scan is a
starting point, not a conclusion, since Brakeman cannot see authorization or
tenant-isolation logic. Citing the Brakeman gate is allowed and expected.

The task file must carry a "Files to read before reviewing" section, and that
section must name $SQUAD_DIR/checklist.security.md as a file Advisor MUST read
before reviewing. Reading it is not optional and it is not summarised for
Advisor by you. In a delta round that section also names delta_file and
changed_files_file. Never name checklist.correctness.md or checklist.merged.md
here.

This task text must NOT mention, quote, summarize or point at the CORRECTNESS
result in any way, in this round or any earlier one. Write it as if the
correctness lens had never run. That applies to the carried-over findings above
with full force: a delta round hands each lens ONLY its own previous findings.
The delta file itself is shared evidence, like the Brakeman gate, because it is
the change - not a review of it. The Brakeman gate is shared evidence too, so
referencing it does not break the independence of the two lenses.

Open this task file with the output contract above; the contract stays FIRST in
the file, ahead of the files-to-read section and everything else. Then run:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt' advisor '$SQUAD_DIR/run-review $SQUAD_DIR/review-security-task.txt $SQUAD_DIR/review-security-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt $review_security_event $SQUAD_DIR/review-schema.json $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt security'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$review_security_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt' advisor '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Advisor security review'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-result.txt'

### Record a lens that did not run this round

merge-reviews needs a result file for BOTH lenses every round. When a lens did
not run, the helper below writes that result. NEVER write one by hand: a
hand-written lens result is a fabricated review, and it reaches the GUI as a
pass.

A lens is SKIPPED when review-scope.txt says so - that is, when the skip rule
above found the diff to be documentation or media only. The reason is one short
line:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/write-lens-result' security skipped '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-result.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt' <round> 'docs/media-only diff per review-scope.txt'

A lens is CARRIED FORWARD only when it returned a clean pass in an earlier round
AND nothing relevant to it has changed since. Since Coder edits code to fix
findings, that is rare: re-running is the default and the safe answer, and when
in doubt you re-run. Pass the round it is carried FROM:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/write-lens-result' correctness carried '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-result.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt' <round> <from-round>

The helper re-checks the previous result before carrying it: a result that is
not a clean pass this lens itself produced is refused, and you must re-run that
lens. It also refuses to carry a skip forward. A lens that previously returned
must_fix, a non-zero status, or a malformed result can NEVER be carried - re-run
it.

Skipping and carrying change nothing about independence. Never mention the other
lens's findings when you re-run, carry or skip a lens.

### Merge the two reviews (mandatory)

Run this after BOTH lens results exist for this round - whether each one was
run, carried forward or skipped - before you decide anything:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/merge-reviews' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-result.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-correctness-status.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-result.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-security-status.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-result.txt' '$SQUAD_DIR/review-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/review-result.txt'

Run it in YOUR OWN pane, not through send-to: it is a deterministic local merge,
not agent work, so it needs no run-wait and no tmux event. It writes the
aggregate the squad GUI reads: status 0 only when both lens statuses are 0,
verdict must_fix when either lens says must_fix, findings unioned and tagged
with the lens that raised them.

When a lens was skipped or carried forward, the aggregate summary opens with a
"[lens states]" line naming it, so the aggregate never reads like two fresh
passes. Say the same thing to PO in your own report.

If merge-reviews fails it says so loudly and writes a must_fix aggregate. That
means a lens result is missing or malformed: re-run that lens, and report it to
PO if it keeps failing. Never hand-write the aggregate files to work around it.

Once merge-reviews has run, capture the review baseline for this round (see
above). Do it whatever the verdict was: the next round's delta starts here.

### When the review round cap is reached

classify-diff reports final_round=yes on review round $REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS, the
last round SQUAD_REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS allows. When that round's review is done and
anything is still outstanding, do NOT ask Coder for another fix round. Instead:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' mark 'review round cap reached'

then report to PO in Japanese: the outstanding must_fix, should_fix and
test_gaps findings from both lens result files with the lens that raised each,
the tests that were run and their results, and the RuboCop and Brakeman gate
results. Say plainly that the round cap stopped the loop and ask PO how to
proceed. Never raise the cap yourself.

### Ask Coder to fix review findings

Write an English request to $SQUAD_DIR/coder-task.txt. Include the UNION of the
must_fix findings from BOTH lens result files, saying which lens raised each
one, plus any RuboCop or Brakeman failure, and require updated tests, RuboCop
and Brakeman. When Brakeman failed, quote the offending warnings from
$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-result.txt and require a real fix - never a narrowed scan. Reiterate that Coder must not
create additional git worktrees and must keep all work in $WORKDIR. Reiterate
that Coder must not run tmux kill-server, tmux kill-session, tmux kill-window,
or tmux kill-pane, including for test cleanup. Continue the exact Coder session:

  '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt' coder '$SQUAD_DIR/run-coder $SQUAD_DIR/coder-task.txt $SQUAD_DIR/coder-result.txt $SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt $coder_event $CODER_SESSION_ID $SQUAD_DIR/coder-schema.json resume $SQUAD_DIR/title.txt'
  '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '$coder_event' '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt' coder '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' 'Coder fix'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-status.txt'
  cat '$SQUAD_DIR/coder-result.txt'

## Workflow

0. Run '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' clear at the start of every turn, including this one.
1. Inspect the linked ticket or task context and update the provisional title.
2. Ask Advisor for a structured implementation plan.
3. Run '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' mark 'plan approval', then show the plan to PO in
   Japanese and ask for approval. Waiting for plan approval is the most common
   place this squad sits idle without PO knowing, so the mark is not optional.
4. Ask Coder to implement the approved plan.
5. Read Coder status and structured verification report.
6. Run the mandatory RuboCop helper and require status 0.
7. Run the mandatory Brakeman helper and require status 0.
8. Run classify-diff to get this round's scope, round number and review_mode.
9. Ask Advisor for the CORRECTNESS review at the scope review_mode gives - the
   full diff in round 1, the delta plus that lens's own previous findings later
   - unless the scope file skips it or it is genuinely carried forward. Record a
   skip or a carry with write-lens-result.
10. Ask Advisor for the SECURITY review of the same change, independently, under
   the same rule. It runs even when the correctness lens returned must_fix, and
   its task text never mentions the correctness result or its findings.
11. Run merge-reviews to write the aggregate files for the GUI.
12. Run capture-review-baseline for this round, so the next round can review
   only what changes from here.
13. If either lens status is non-zero, either lens verdict is must_fix, or
   RuboCop or Brakeman failed, ask Coder to fix the issues - UNLESS classify-diff
   reported final_round=yes, in which case mark and report to PO instead of
   starting another fix round. Pass the union of the findings from both lenses.
14. After every fix, repeat from step 5.
15. Report completion to PO in Japanese. Include the squad worktree path, branch,
   test commands and results, the mandatory RuboCop and Brakeman results, and
   any lens that was skipped, carried forward, or reviewed at delta scope.
PROMPT
}

# The resumed Manager keeps its previous Claude session, so its transcript still
# contains the pane IDs it used before the resume. Those IDs are dead: say so
# explicitly, or the model will keep reusing them from its own history.
generate_resume_prompt() {
  cat "$SQUAD_DIR/manager-prompt.md" > "$SQUAD_DIR/manager-resume-prompt.md"
  cat >> "$SQUAD_DIR/manager-resume-prompt.md" << PROMPT

## MIGRATION: this squad was resumed (read this before any command)

This squad has been resumed in a NEW tmux window, so every tmux pane ID changed.
Any literal tmux pane ID (a %-prefixed number) that appears in earlier turns of
this conversation is now STALE, and tmux may already have reassigned it to an
unrelated pane.

- Those earlier commands are OBSOLETE. NEVER copy or reuse a command containing
  a literal pane ID, and never run 'tmux send-keys' yourself.
- NEVER pass a pane ID to run-wait. It takes a role: manager, coder, or advisor.
- ALWAYS address agents by role through the helpers, exactly as shown in the
  Commands section above:

    '$SQUAD_DIR/send-to' --status-file '<status file>' coder '<command>'
    '$SQUAD_DIR/run-wait' '<event>' '<status file>' coder '$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS' '<label>'

- When you START an agent step, always pass --status-file, naming the SAME
  status file the following run-wait reads. It clears that file in your pane
  before the keystrokes are queued, which is what stops run-wait from handing
  you the PREVIOUS round's result. Never pass it to run-wait. Use the
  no-option form only for a one-off command that writes no status file.

- The helpers resolve the live pane for this run themselves. If one reports that
  a role cannot be resolved, report that to PO and stop; do not guess.
- The Coder and Advisor panes of this resumed window are fresh shells: no agent
  is mid-task, and the Coder resumes its own session only when you send
  run-coder with the resume mode.
- A resume does NOT answer any question you had asked PO, so an awaiting-PO
  marker deliberately survived into this window. Your first action is still
  '$SQUAD_DIR/await-po' clear - run it, then look at whether PO has actually
  replied, and mark again if you are still waiting.
PROMPT
}

resume_run() {
  local new_window
  local target_session="${2:-}"

  # Without an explicit target session, resume must run inside tmux (existing
  # behavior). With --session, we create the window in that session directly and
  # do not require $TMUX (e.g. when launched from an external tool).
  if [[ -z "$target_session" && -z "${TMUX:-}" ]]; then
    echo "Error: squad resume must be run inside tmux (or pass --session <name>)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  # Resume splits panes too, so the same check has to happen before the new
  # window is created rather than when the checklist is regenerated below.
  require_readable_checklist_config

  # An EXPLICIT run-id must match exactly. Without this, load_run's fallback
  # silently resolves a typo'd id to the most recent retained run - and resume
  # does not just display that run, it attaches LIVE AGENTS to it. A Coder would
  # start editing another squad's worktree and branch, which is far worse than
  # the wrong thing merely being shown. `close`, `rm` and `base` all already
  # refuse a bad id; resume was the only one that did not.
  #
  # Only the NO-ARG form may resolve on its own: the current window's run first,
  # then the latest.
  if [[ -n "${1:-}" ]]; then
    load_run "$1" false
  else
    load_run "" true
  fi
  [[ -d "$WORKDIR" ]] || {
    echo "Error: squad worktree no longer exists: $WORKDIR" >&2
    exit 1
  }

  if window_exists "${WINDOW_ID:-}"; then
    echo "Error: squad is already running in window $WINDOW_ID" >&2
    exit 1
  fi

  # Diagnostics only: kept so state.env still records the original layout.
  ORIG_MANAGER_PANE="${ORIG_MANAGER_PANE:-${MANAGER_PANE:-}}"
  ORIG_CODER_PANE="${ORIG_CODER_PANE:-${CODER_PANE:-}}"
  ORIG_ADVISOR_PANE="${ORIG_ADVISOR_PANE:-${ADVISOR_PANE:-}}"

  if [[ -n "$target_session" ]]; then
    new_window="$(tmux new-window -d -t "${target_session}:" -c "$WORKDIR" -P -F '#{window_id} #{pane_id}')"
  else
    new_window="$(tmux new-window -d -c "$WORKDIR" -P -F '#{window_id} #{pane_id}')"
  fi
  read -r WINDOW_ID MANAGER_PANE <<< "$new_window"
  CODER_PANE="$(tmux split-window -h -c "$WORKDIR" -d -t "$MANAGER_PANE" -P -F '#{pane_id}')"
  ADVISOR_PANE="$(tmux split-window -v -c "$WORKDIR" -d -t "$CODER_PANE" -P -F '#{pane_id}')"
  CURRENT_WINDOW_NAME="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$WINDOW_ID" '#W')"
  # Restore the name the Manager last set, exactly as the pane title is restored
  # from title.txt below. state.env's copy is the next best thing (it is written
  # on every state change), and the placeholder is the last resort.
  if [[ -s "$SQUAD_DIR/short-name.txt" ]]; then
    WINDOW_SHORT_NAME="$(head -n 1 "$SQUAD_DIR/short-name.txt")"
  fi
  WINDOW_SHORT_NAME="${WINDOW_SHORT_NAME:-$(placeholder_window_name)}"
  RUN_STATUS="running"
  write_state
  tag_all_panes

  # Regenerate the helper scripts and prompts from this version of squad, so a
  # run created by an older version is migrated to role addressing the moment it
  # is resumed. The prompts contain no pane ID, so no rewrite is needed.
  SQUAD_TITLE="$(cat "$SQUAD_DIR/title.txt" 2>/dev/null || printf 'squad')"
  # Re-emitted from state.env before the helpers, so brakeman-command.txt exists
  # for a run created before the Brakeman gate did (where it resolves to the
  # empty command, i.e. skip) and so launch and resume stay idempotent.
  printf '%s\n' "${BRAKEMAN_COMMAND:-}" > "$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-command.txt"
  # Same reason: a squad created before the review round cap existed has no
  # REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS in state.env, and the Manager prompt interpolates it. The
  # recorded value wins over the environment, exactly as WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
  # does, so resuming never silently changes the cap the run started with.
  REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS="${REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS:-$SQUAD_REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS_DEFAULT}"
  # Drop the review baseline, so the FIRST review after a resume reads the FULL
  # diff again.
  #
  # Deliberately by construction rather than by luck. A resumed Manager has lost
  # the conversation it built its judgement in, and in a delta round it would be
  # handed a small diff plus "the findings from last round" that it can no
  # longer see. That is precisely where a delta review is weakest. It also
  # covers the case where the working tree moved while the squad was closed. One
  # extra full review per resume is a price worth paying; resumes are rare.
  rm -f "$SQUAD_DIR/review-baseline.txt"
  # Regenerated on resume for the same reason the helpers are: a run created
  # before a schema existed gets it as soon as it is resumed.
  generate_schemas
  # Re-resolved before the prompt, so a resumed run reviews against the project
  # checklist as it stands NOW, not as it stood when the squad was created.
  generate_review_checklist
  generate_helper_scripts
  generate_manager_prompt
  generate_resume_prompt

  # PRESERVES an existing awaiting-PO marker: a resume does not answer the
  # Manager's question, so the badge must come back with the window. The base is
  # the one just restored above, and window-name re-composes the marker onto it.
  WINDOW_DISPLAY_NAME="$(
    "$SQUAD_DIR/window-name" display "$WINDOW_SHORT_NAME" 2>/dev/null ||
      printf '%s' "$WINDOW_SHORT_NAME"
  )"
  tmux rename-window -t "$WINDOW_ID" "$WINDOW_DISPLAY_NAME"
  tmux select-pane -t "$MANAGER_PANE" -T "$SQUAD_TITLE"
  tmux select-pane -t "$CODER_PANE" -T "Coder"
  tmux select-pane -t "$ADVISOR_PANE" -T "Advisor"
  tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" pane-border-status top
  tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" pane-border-format " #{pane_title} "
  tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" @squad_run_id "$RUN_ID"
  tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" @squad_state_file "$STATE_FILE"
  tmux send-keys -t "$MANAGER_PANE" "$SQUAD_DIR/run-manager-resume '$WORKDIR' '$SQUAD_DIR/manager-resume-prompt.md' '$MANAGER_SESSION_ID'" Enter
  tmux select-window -t "$WINDOW_ID"
  printf 'Squad resumed: %s\n' "$RUN_ID"
}

[[ $# -gt 0 ]] || usage
case "$1" in
  -h|--help)
    usage 0
    ;;
  list)
    list_runs
    exit 0
    ;;
  close)
    close_run "${2:-}"
    exit 0
    ;;
  rm)
    shift
    [[ $# -gt 0 ]] || {
      echo "Error: squad rm requires at least one run-id (see: squad list)" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    rm_status=0
    for run_id in "$@"; do
      rm_state_file="$SQUAD_RUN_ROOT/$run_id/state.env"
      if [[ ! -f "$rm_state_file" ]]; then
        echo "Error: unknown squad run: $run_id" >&2
        rm_status=1
        continue
      fi
      remove_run "$rm_state_file" || rm_status=1
    done
    exit "$rm_status"
    ;;
  clean)
    clean_runs
    exit 0
    ;;
  base)
    shift
    base_target_run=""
    # `--run` names another squad. A POSITIONAL run-id is deliberately not
    # accepted: `squad base <x>` would be ambiguous between "inspect run x" and
    # "set the base to ref x".
    if [[ "${1:-}" == "--run" ]]; then
      if [[ $# -lt 2 || -z "${2:-}" ]]; then
        echo "Error: --run requires a run-id (see: squad list)" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
      base_target_run="$2"
      shift 2
    fi
    if [[ $# -gt 1 ]]; then
      echo "Error: squad base takes at most one ref (see: squad --help)" >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    if [[ $# -eq 1 ]]; then
      base_run "$base_target_run" "$1" true
    else
      base_run "$base_target_run" "" false
    fi
    exit 0
    ;;
  resume)
    shift
    resume_session=""
    if [[ "${1:-}" == "--session" ]]; then
      if [[ $# -lt 2 || -z "${2:-}" ]]; then
        echo "Error: --session requires a value" >&2
        exit 1
      fi
      resume_session="$2"
      shift 2
    fi
    resume_run "${1:-}" "$resume_session"
    exit 0
    ;;
  gen-helpers)
    [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] || {
      echo "Error: squad gen-helpers requires a target directory" >&2
      exit 1
    }
    # Writes the WHOLE generated surface - helpers, schemas, checklist and the
    # Manager prompts - so all of it can be inspected or tested without a tmux
    # server, a worktree, a pane or a single agent. The placeholder values below
    # only ever reach the generated text.
    require_own_gen_dir "$2"
    SQUAD_DIR="$2"
    mkdir -p "$SQUAD_DIR"
    RUN_ID="${SQUAD_GEN_RUN_ID:-gen-helpers}"
    REPO_ROOT="${SQUAD_GEN_REPO_ROOT:-$(pwd)}"
    WORKDIR="${SQUAD_GEN_WORKDIR:-$REPO_ROOT}"
    BRANCH="${SQUAD_GEN_BRANCH:-feat/squad-$RUN_ID}"
    SQUAD_TITLE="${SQUAD_GEN_TITLE:-gen-helpers}"
    TICKET_ID="${SQUAD_GEN_TICKET_ID:-}"
    MANAGER_SESSION_ID="${SQUAD_GEN_MANAGER_SESSION_ID:-00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000}"
    CODER_SESSION_ID="${SQUAD_GEN_CODER_SESSION_ID:-00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001}"
    WINDOW_SHORT_NAME="${SQUAD_GEN_SHORT_NAME:-$(placeholder_window_name)}"
    WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS="${SQUAD_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-7200}"
    [[ "$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
      echo "Error: SQUAD_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS must be a positive integer." >&2
      exit 1
    }
    resolve_review_max_rounds
    # set_worktree_mode reads WORKDIR against REPO_ROOT, so it must come after
    # both are assigned.
    set_worktree_mode
    # TOCTOU narrowing, deliberately cheap rather than airtight.
    #
    # The check above is a check-then-use, and nothing holds the directory in
    # between: with a writable parent, a symlink can be raced into place at one
    # of the fixed output names after the check and before the write. Two
    # mitigations, both one line:
    #
    #   * the check is REPEATED here, immediately before the first write, so the
    #     window is the generation itself rather than the whole argument parse;
    #   * noclobber makes every `>` below REFUSE a path that already exists,
    #     which is what a symlink raced into place resolves to.
    #
    # RESIDUAL RACE, knowingly left open: a symlink raced in after this second
    # check whose target does NOT yet exist is still followed, and the target
    # created through it. Closing that needs O_NOFOLLOW on every open, which
    # bash cannot express. gen-helpers is a developer-facing test entry point,
    # and this is where the PO drew the line - do not read it as hardened.
    require_own_gen_dir "$SQUAD_DIR"
    set -o noclobber
    printf '%s\n' "${SQUAD_GEN_RUBOCOP_COMMAND:-}" > "$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-command.txt"
    printf '%s\n' "${SQUAD_GEN_BRAKEMAN_COMMAND:-}" > "$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-command.txt"
    printf '%s\n' "$SQUAD_TITLE" > "$SQUAD_DIR/title.txt"
    generate_schemas
    generate_review_checklist
    generate_helper_scripts
    generate_manager_prompt
    generate_resume_prompt
    printf 'Generated helpers, schemas, checklist and prompts in %s\n' "$SQUAD_DIR"
    exit 0
    ;;
esac

TASK="$*"
if [[ "$TASK" =~ ([[:alnum:]_]+-[[:digit:]]+) ]]; then
  TICKET_ID="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
  SQUAD_TITLE="$TICKET_ID | チケット内容を確認中"
else
  TICKET_ID=""
  SQUAD_TITLE="チケット内容を確認中"
fi
WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS="${SQUAD_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-7200}"
[[ "$WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || {
  echo "Error: SQUAD_WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS must be a positive integer." >&2
  exit 1
}
resolve_review_max_rounds
ORIGINAL_WORKDIR="$(pwd)"

if [[ -z "${TMUX:-}" ]]; then
  echo "Error: starting a squad needs either a tmux session or a herdr pane." >&2
  echo "       In tmux, squad splits the current pane into three agents." >&2
  echo "       In a herdr pane (HERDR_PANE_ID set), squad hands off to squad-herdr." >&2
  echo "       Neither was detected here." >&2
  exit 1
fi

for cmd in claude codex git jq tmux uuidgen; do
  command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    echo "Error: $cmd is not installed or not in PATH" >&2
    exit 1
  }
done

# Before the worktree, the run directory and the pane splits below.
require_readable_checklist_config

REPO_ROOT="$(git -C "$ORIGINAL_WORKDIR" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || {
  echo "Error: squad must be run inside a git repository" >&2
  exit 1
}

# git worktree add creates the worktree from HEAD, so a repository without any
# commits (unborn HEAD) cannot get an isolated worktree. In that case fall back
# to running the squad directly in the original repository.
HAS_COMMITS=true
git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD >/dev/null || HAS_COMMITS=false

# --- Setup isolated worktree and artifact directory ---------------------------

RUN_ID="$(date '+%Y%m%d-%H%M%S')-$$"
STARTED_AT="$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
REPO_NAME="$(basename "$REPO_ROOT")"
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE="$(
  printf '%s' "$REPO_NAME" |
    tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' |
    sed -E 's/[^a-z0-9_-]+/-/g; s/^-+//; s/-+$//'
)"
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE="${COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE:-squad}"
SQUAD_DIR="$SQUAD_RUN_ROOT/$RUN_ID"
if [[ "$HAS_COMMITS" == "true" ]]; then
  WORKDIR="$SQUAD_WORKTREE_ROOT/$REPO_NAME-$RUN_ID"
  if [[ -n "$TICKET_ID" ]]; then
    BRANCH="feat/$TICKET_ID"
    if branch_exists "$BRANCH"; then
      BRANCH="feat/$TICKET_ID-$RUN_ID"
    fi
  else
    BRANCH="feat/squad-$RUN_ID"
  fi
else
  # No commits yet: work directly in the original repository on its current
  # (unborn) branch.
  WORKDIR="$REPO_ROOT"
  BRANCH="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" symbolic-ref --short -q HEAD || echo main)"
fi
MANAGER_SESSION_ID="$(uuidgen | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
CODER_SESSION_ID="$(uuidgen | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"

# If the launch dies anywhere between creating the run directory and actually
# starting the Manager, leave nothing behind: no orphaned run directory and no
# orphaned worktree/branch.
#
# The guard is this flag, NOT "does state.env exist". state.env is written well
# BEFORE the Manager launch, so every abort in that window found it already
# present and returned without cleaning anything - the run directory and a
# still-registered worktree were both left behind. Such a run is not usefully
# resumable either: `squad resume` re-execs `claude --resume
# "$MANAGER_SESSION_ID"`, and that session is only created by the launch that
# never happened.
#
# squad is deliberately MORE conservative than vet here. squad retains its
# worktree by design and creates a branch, and deleting something the PO wants
# is far worse than leaving an orphan. So this only ever removes resources squad
# itself just created, only before the launch is committed, and never touches
# the original repository.
SQUAD_LAUNCH_COMMITTED=false

cleanup_failed_launch() {
  # Preserve the real exit status. The old handler ended in `return 0`, which
  # replaced the failing status, so a failed launch reported success.
  rc=$?
  trap - EXIT
  if [[ "$SQUAD_LAUNCH_COMMITTED" == "true" ]]; then
    exit "$rc"
  fi
  # $? alone is not trustworthy. Measured on bash 3.2: after a `set -u`
  # unbound-variable abort, $? is 0 inside the EXIT trap even though the shell is
  # dying, while `false` gives 1 and `exit 3` gives 3 as expected. Reaching this
  # branch means the Manager was never launched, which is itself proof of
  # failure, so force a non-zero status when $? did not supply one.
  [[ "$rc" -ne 0 ]] || rc=1

  echo "" >&2
  echo "squad: launch failed before the Manager started (exit $rc); cleaning up." >&2

  # ORIGINAL-REPOSITORY MODE: squad created no worktree and no branch, and
  # WORKDIR is the PO's own repository. Never delete repository files, the
  # worktree, or the user's branch here - there is nothing of squad's to remove
  # beyond its own artifact directory.
  if [[ "${WORKDIR:-}" == "${REPO_ROOT:-}" ]]; then
    echo "squad: original-repository mode - your repository, worktree and branch were left untouched." >&2
  elif [[ -n "${WORKDIR:-}" && -d "$WORKDIR" ]]; then
    if git -C "$REPO_ROOT" worktree remove --force "$WORKDIR" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "squad: removed worktree $WORKDIR" >&2
    else
      # Leave it rather than rm -rf a directory git still tracks: a half-removed
      # worktree the PO can finish removing beats deleting something unexpected.
      echo "squad: could NOT remove worktree $WORKDIR" >&2
      echo "squad: remove it yourself with: git -C '$REPO_ROOT' worktree remove --force '$WORKDIR'" >&2
    fi
    git -C "$REPO_ROOT" worktree prune >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    # Only ever the branch squad just created for this run.
    if [[ -n "${BRANCH:-}" ]] && git -C "$REPO_ROOT" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$BRANCH"; then
      if git -C "$REPO_ROOT" branch -D "$BRANCH" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "squad: removed branch $BRANCH" >&2
      else
        echo "squad: left branch $BRANCH in place; delete it with: git -C '$REPO_ROOT' branch -D '$BRANCH'" >&2
      fi
    fi
  fi

  if [[ -n "${SQUAD_DIR:-}" && -d "$SQUAD_DIR" ]]; then
    rm -rf "$SQUAD_DIR"
    echo "squad: removed run directory $SQUAD_DIR" >&2
  fi
  exit "$rc"
}
trap cleanup_failed_launch EXIT

mkdir -p "$SQUAD_RUN_ROOT" "$SQUAD_WORKTREE_ROOT" "$SQUAD_DIR"

if [[ "$HAS_COMMITS" == "true" && -n "$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" status --porcelain)" ]]; then
  echo "Note: existing changes remain untouched in the original worktree:"
  git -C "$REPO_ROOT" status --short
  echo ""
fi

if [[ -n "${SQUAD_RUBOCOP_COMMAND:-}" ]]; then
  RUBOCOP_COMMAND="$SQUAD_RUBOCOP_COMMAND"
elif [[ ! -f "$REPO_ROOT/Gemfile" ]]; then
  # Not a Ruby project: run-rubocop sees the empty command and reports the
  # step as skipped instead of failing the squad.
  RUBOCOP_COMMAND=""
elif [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/compose.yml" ]] && command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  RUBOCOP_COMMAND="COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=$COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE docker compose run --no-deps --rm --entrypoint '' app bundle exec rubocop --force-exclusion --fail-level W --display-only-fail-level-offenses"
elif [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/compose.ci.yml" ]] && command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  RUBOCOP_COMMAND="COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=$COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE docker compose -f compose.ci.yml run --no-deps --rm --entrypoint '' app bundle exec rubocop --force-exclusion --fail-level W --display-only-fail-level-offenses"
elif command -v bundle >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  RUBOCOP_COMMAND="bundle exec rubocop --force-exclusion --fail-level W --display-only-fail-level-offenses"
else
  echo "Error: no RuboCop command could be detected for this Ruby project." >&2
  echo "Set SQUAD_RUBOCOP_COMMAND to the project-specific command." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# Brakeman detection mirrors RuboCop's shape and ordering, with two deliberate
# differences:
#
#   * The bare branch is PROBED (`bundle exec brakeman --version`) instead of
#     merely checking that bundler exists. RuboCop is in practically every Rails
#     Gemfile; Brakeman is not, and an unprobed command would turn every Ruby
#     project without the gem into a hard failure.
#   * There is no error/exit fallback. Brakeman-not-installed SKIPS with status
#     0. A missing scanner must never block a squad; a scanner that runs and
#     finds something must always block it.
#
# The scan itself is strict and whole-app (see run-brakeman): 0 warnings
# required, with the project's own config/brakeman.ignore carrying any curated
# pre-existing warnings. A project needing a different policy sets
# SQUAD_BRAKEMAN_COMMAND.
if [[ -n "${SQUAD_BRAKEMAN_COMMAND:-}" ]]; then
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND="$SQUAD_BRAKEMAN_COMMAND"
elif [[ ! -f "$REPO_ROOT/Gemfile" ]]; then
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND=""
elif [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/compose.yml" ]] && command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND="COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=$COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE docker compose run --no-deps --rm --entrypoint '' app bundle exec brakeman"
elif [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/compose.ci.yml" ]] && command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND="COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=$COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME_SAFE docker compose -f compose.ci.yml run --no-deps --rm --entrypoint '' app bundle exec brakeman"
elif command -v bundle >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
  (cd "$REPO_ROOT" && bundle exec brakeman --version >/dev/null 2>&1); then
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND="bundle exec brakeman"
else
  # Ruby project without Brakeman available: skip, never block.
  BRAKEMAN_COMMAND=""
fi

if [[ "$HAS_COMMITS" == "true" ]]; then
  # Record the fork point BEFORE creating the worktree. `worktree add ... HEAD`
  # branches from exactly this commit, so it IS the base of every diff for this
  # run — no base-branch heuristic can be more accurate. Captured immediately
  # before the add so no later command can observe a moved HEAD.
  SQUAD_BASE_COMMIT="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse --verify "HEAD^{commit}")"
  # Display only. A detached HEAD still has a perfectly good commit above; only
  # the human-readable label falls back (branch -> exact tag -> short sha).
  SQUAD_BASE_REF="$(
    git -C "$REPO_ROOT" symbolic-ref --quiet --short HEAD 2>/dev/null ||
      git -C "$REPO_ROOT" describe --tags --exact-match HEAD 2>/dev/null ||
      git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null ||
      printf ''
  )"
  git -C "$REPO_ROOT" worktree add -b "$BRANCH" "$WORKDIR" HEAD
  if [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/.env" ]]; then
    cp "$REPO_ROOT/.env" "$WORKDIR/.env"
  fi
else
  # Unborn HEAD: the squad runs directly in the original repository and no
  # branch is created, so there is no fork point that could exist yet. Both keys
  # stay EMPTY rather than recording a fiction; cockpit reads empty as "not
  # recorded" and falls back to its own detection.
  SQUAD_BASE_COMMIT=""
  SQUAD_BASE_REF=""
  echo "Note: this repository has no commits yet, so no isolated worktree is created."
  echo "The squad works directly in: $WORKDIR"
  echo ""
fi

set_worktree_mode

cat > "$SQUAD_DIR/run-info.txt" << INFO
Squad title:       $SQUAD_TITLE
Ticket ID:         ${TICKET_ID:-not detected}
Started at:        $STARTED_AT
Original worktree: $REPO_ROOT
Squad worktree:    $WORKDIR
Worktree mode:     $WORKTREE_MODE
Squad branch:      $BRANCH
Artifacts:         $SQUAD_DIR
Manager session:   $MANAGER_SESSION_ID
Coder session:     $CODER_SESSION_ID
RuboCop command:   ${RUBOCOP_COMMAND:-(none: not a Ruby project, RuboCop step is skipped)}
Brakeman command:  ${BRAKEMAN_COMMAND:-(none: not a Ruby project or Brakeman not installed, Brakeman step is skipped)}
Wait timeout:      $WAIT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS seconds
Review rounds:     $REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS maximum
INFO

cat "$SQUAD_DIR/run-info.txt"
echo ""
printf '%s\n' "$SQUAD_TITLE" > "$SQUAD_DIR/title.txt"

# --- Create panes -------------------------------------------------------------

MANAGER_PANE="${TMUX_PANE:-$(tmux display-message -p '#{pane_id}')}"
CURRENT_WINDOW_NAME="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$MANAGER_PANE" '#W')"
WINDOW_ID="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$MANAGER_PANE" '#{window_id}')"
WINDOW_SHORT_NAME="$(placeholder_window_name)"
# Seeded now so `squad resume` has a name to restore even if the Manager never
# reaches its first title update.
printf '%s\n' "$WINDOW_SHORT_NAME" > "$SQUAD_DIR/short-name.txt"
CODER_PANE=$(tmux split-window -h -c "$WORKDIR" -d -t "$MANAGER_PANE" -P -F '#{pane_id}')
ADVISOR_PANE=$(tmux split-window -v -c "$WORKDIR" -d -t "$CODER_PANE" -P -F '#{pane_id}')

RUN_STATUS="running"
write_state
tag_all_panes
tmux rename-window -t "$WINDOW_ID" "$WINDOW_SHORT_NAME"
tmux select-pane -t "$MANAGER_PANE" -T "$SQUAD_TITLE"
tmux select-pane -t "$CODER_PANE" -T "Coder"
tmux select-pane -t "$ADVISOR_PANE" -T "Advisor"
tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" pane-border-status top
tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" pane-border-format " #{pane_title} "
tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" @squad_run_id "$RUN_ID"
tmux set-option -w -t "$WINDOW_ID" @squad_state_file "$STATE_FILE"

# --- Structured output schemas ------------------------------------------------

generate_schemas

printf '%s\n' "$RUBOCOP_COMMAND" > "$SQUAD_DIR/rubocop-command.txt"
printf '%s\n' "$BRAKEMAN_COMMAND" > "$SQUAD_DIR/brakeman-command.txt"

# --- Generated helper scripts and Manager prompt ------------------------------
# All three are regenerated verbatim by resume_run, so they must never contain a
# tmux pane ID.

generate_review_checklist
generate_helper_scripts
generate_manager_prompt

# --- Launch Manager -----------------------------------------------------------

cd "$WORKDIR"
MANAGER_GUARD_BIN="$SQUAD_DIR/manager-guard-bin"
mkdir -p "$MANAGER_GUARD_BIN"
SQUAD_REAL_TMUX="$(command -v tmux)"
export SQUAD_REAL_TMUX
cat > "$MANAGER_GUARD_BIN/tmux" <<'WRAPPER'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
for arg in "$@"; do
  case "$arg" in
    kill-server|kill-session|kill-window|kill-pane)
      printf 'squad: blocked dangerous tmux command: %s\n' "$arg" >&2
      exit 126
      ;;
  esac
done
exec "$SQUAD_REAL_TMUX" "$@"
WRAPPER
chmod +x "$MANAGER_GUARD_BIN/tmux"
export PATH="$MANAGER_GUARD_BIN:$PATH"

# Everything the launch needed has succeeded; from here the squad is the PO's to
# keep. The Manager may still fail or be interrupted, but the worktree, branch
# and artifacts must survive that so the PO can inspect, commit, merge or resume.
SQUAD_LAUNCH_COMMITTED=true

set +e
printf '[%s] Manager prompt sent for squad: %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')" "$SQUAD_TITLE"
CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1 claude --dangerously-skip-permissions \
  --allowedTools "Bash Read Glob Grep WebFetch WebSearch" \
  --append-system-prompt "$(cat "$SQUAD_DIR/manager-prompt.md")" \
  --session-id "$MANAGER_SESSION_ID" \
  "$TASK"
MANAGER_STATUS=$?
set -e

# The Manager is gone, so nothing is waiting on PO any more. Without this a run
# that ended while a question was outstanding would leave the marker up forever:
# there is no Manager turn left to clear it, and cockpit would keep showing the
# badge for a squad that is not running.
#
# This pane is still alive - claude exited, this script did not - so the window
# is renameable and the badge really does come down on screen. Going through
# clear_awaiting_po rather than rm is what guarantees that; see the invariant on
# that function.
clear_awaiting_po

echo ""
echo "Manager exited with status $MANAGER_STATUS."
echo "Squad worktree retained at: $WORKDIR"
echo "Squad branch retained as:   $BRANCH"
echo "Artifacts retained at:      $SQUAD_DIR"
exit "$MANAGER_STATUS"
